0.12 (unreleased) — Qt 6 port + feature rebuild
Core / Qt 6 port:
- Complete port from Qt4/WebKit to Qt 6/WebEngine: WebView, WebPage, tab
  widget, browser window, application object (MIG02–MIG15).
- qmake build retargeted to Qt 6 (webenginewidgets, webchannel,
  uitools, core5compat); WebKit and Qt4 conditionals removed (MIG01).
- Cookie jar on QWebEngineCookieStore with block/allow exception lists
  and third-party-cookie policy (MIG03).
- Slimmed QNetworkAccessManager kept for app fetches only; web traffic
  belongs to Chromium (MIG04).
- file:// directory listings via the arora-file URL scheme (MIG04).
- History store, dialog, menu and completer on a named persistent
  profile (MIG06).
- Download manager drives QWebEngineDownloadRequest (MIG05).
- Bookmarks store/model/dialogs/toolbar on Qt 6 (MIG07).
- Preferences, accept-language and Clear Private Data dialogs ported
  (MIG11).
- Autofill via injected autofill.js + hardened WebChannel reporting
  (MIG10).
- Source viewer, syntax highlighter and page search ported (MIG12).
- NPAPI/Flash machinery removed (MIG13).
- Translations regenerated; install + freedesktop rules updated (MIG16).
- Deprecated-API sweep complete (MIG17).
- Bundled CLI tools rebuilt for Qt 6 (TOOL01).
- Qt 6.12 toolchain (QTUP01).
- Session restore end to end (SESS01).
- Session state now survives an OS kill: SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP
  flush the pending session save before the process dies, instead of
  losing changes still inside the debounce window (SESS02). SIGKILL
  remains unflushed by design — it cannot be caught.
- The browser can no longer start windowless or die silently: a
  session restore that yields no windows (empty or corrupt session,
  a declined restore prompt) now opens a normal window instead of
  leaving a running process with nothing to show, and a stray
  surviving dialog can no longer keep the process alive after the
  last browser window is gone (SESS02). Every exit is also
  accounted for — the next start reports whether the previous run
  exited cleanly, was terminated by a signal, or died uncleanly, so
  a crash never masquerades as a normal quit or vice versa.
- Command-line URL arguments treated as untrusted input (SEC09).
- GPU crash safe mode: Qt 6.12's WebEngine can trap (SIGTRAP) on a
  display-compositor CHECK on some driver stacks, killing the whole
  browser. Arora now arms Chromium's --disable-gpu-compositing —
  which keeps WebGL/canvas acceleration while moving page compositing
  to software — after two consecutive unclean exits, and exposes the
  same mitigation as a Preferences checkbox and the
  ARORA_DISABLE_GPU_COMPOSITING env override (CRASH01).
- Printing can no longer kill the browser: an accepted print dialog
  describing an unprintable target (no configured printer, a file
  output path that can't be written) is refused with a readable error
  instead of being handed to the engine, and closing Print Preview
  mid-job now waits for the in-flight print instead of painting into
  a dead printer. Fatal exits are also observable now — a crash
  reporter writes a marker + backtrace to stderr and
  crashreport.log on SIGSEGV/SIGABRT and uncaught exceptions
  (CRASH02).
- Print Preview shows the real document again instead of a blank
  gray "1 / 0" pane: the stock preview dialog needed synchronous
  painting into its printer, which engine printing can't do, so the
  preview now asks the page to produce its actual print PDF
  asynchronously and renders that in the shell's own PDF view —
  true pagination, margins and orientation (Page Setup regenerates),
  plus Save as PDF; Print hands the generated document to a real
  printer through the existing PDF print path. Tabs already showing
  a PDF preview that document, and engines without print support
  (Servo) say so instead of opening an empty dialog (PRINT01).

UI:
- Light/dark follows the desktop scheme; ARORA_COLOR_SCHEME override
  (UIP01).
- Central icon provider + bundled Adwaita/Breeze/Tabler sets, light and
  dark variants (ICONS01).
- Bundled Tabler icons are now the default set — the previous "follow
  the desktop theme" default rendered missing glyphs as duplicate
  lookalikes on sparse themes.  Every chrome icon the browser requests
  ships inside the bundled set (and its dark variant) (ICONS10).
- Lucide joins the bundled icon sets — thin rounded strokes close to
  Firefox's Proton icon language, covering the full 62-name chrome
  surface with a generated dark variant.  Selectable in Preferences >
  Appearance; Tabler stays the default (ICONS11).
- The "Native" (follow-the-desktop-theme) icon option is retired —
  sparse desktop themes served missing glyphs as duplicate
  lookalikes.  Profiles that had it selected migrate to Tabler
  automatically; only the bundled sets remain selectable (ICONS12).
- The bundled "arora-legacy" icon set (the original 2009 artwork) is
  removed — Tabler covers its full name list and is now the fallback
  root every other set inherits.  Nothing user-visible changes;
  profiles pinned to the old set fall back to Tabler (ICONS13).
- Vivaldi-style vertical navigation in Preferences (UIP03).
- Status-bar load indicator and zoom control (UIP04).
- Shield icon at the left of the location bar (UIP05).
- Scrollable Preferences pages, capped to the screen (UIP06).
- Tab bar positions: Top/Bottom/Left/Right (TABS01).
- Left/Right tab strips show horizontal, readable tab titles and can
  be resized by dragging their inner edge (TABS02).
- Pinned tabs: the tab context menu's Pin Tab/Unpin Tab anchors the
  tab in a block at the start of the strip — it keeps its favicon and
  title like any tab but has no close button, middle-click is ignored,
  it is exempt from Close Other Tabs and idle-tab sleeping; new tabs
  open right after the block, dragging a tab across the boundary pins
  or unpins it, and pin state is restored with the session
  (TABS04, TABS14).
- Named, color-coded tab groups (TABGRP01).
- Tab groups gained a Close Group context-menu action that closes
  every member — including a collapsed group's hidden tabs — through
  the normal close path so Recently Closed Tab can restore them, with
  a confirmation naming the real member count above the multi-close
  threshold; and dragging a tab now previews the drop-to-group stack
  by tinting the hovered tab's middle band (or a collapsed group's
  chip) with the group color while the edge quarters keep the reorder
  meaning (TABGRP02).
- Compact tab strip: a container tab no longer inflates the whole
  bar — the container's accent line and name chip now paint inside a
  standard-height tab row instead of stacking a chip band on top of
  it, so the strip stays at normal browser-tab height even when
  container tabs are open (TABS06).
- Undo closed tab: Ctrl+Shift+T (or the tab context menu's Reopen
  Closed Tab, plus a titled/icon'd submenu of recent entries) reopens
  the last closed tab at its original index with history, scroll
  position, tab group, container and pinned state intact. The
  memory-only stack keeps 25 entries per window, never records
  private tabs and is wiped by Clear Private Data (TABS05).
- Resizable widths everywhere a strip meets content: the sidebar's
  inner edge is now a visible drag grip (double-click resets, the
  width is remembered per side and survives restarts, and it follows
  the dock to the left or right edge), Left/Right tab strips gained a
  double-click reset on their existing grip, and horizontal tab
  strips get Minimum/Maximum tab-width controls in Preferences so
  tabs neither collapse nor balloon (RSZ01).
- Slimmer tab strip: per-tab close buttons no longer inflate the row
  and tab height is bounded to icon+text height, bringing the strip
  back to normal browser-tab size (TABS07).
- Tab strip stays compact with container tabs: the container name
  chip no longer reserves a second row — a single container tab used
  to double the whole strip's height.  Container tabs now show the
  container color stripe on the tab's edge with the name in the
  tooltip, and automatic strip thickness is tightened to the
  ~26-28px browser band (TABS08).
- Tor window tab strip: the masked private-browsing favicon on tor
  tabs no longer renders clipped to a sliver — the page-icon widget is
  pinned to the icon box so the badge is fully visible (TOR06).
- Optional sidebar dock: bookmarks/history/downloads/notes (SIDE01).
- Bookmarks > "Show All Bookmarks" (Ctrl+Shift+B) now raises the
  sidebar dock focused on the Bookmarks rail instead of opening a
  separate manager window, and the rail carries Add/Remove buttons —
  Add books the current page through the same dialog the toolbar
  star uses, Remove deletes the selection (also on the rail's
  context menu as "Remove Entry") (SIDE04).
- Command palette, Ctrl+Shift+P (CMD01).
- Tab search dropdown, Ctrl+Shift+A, with hover previews (POL02).
- Copy Clean Link and QR-code sharing (POL01).
- Find-bar n/m match counter; page dark mode; middle-click paste
  (POL03).
- Complete image context menu (CTX01).
- Reader Mode, Ctrl+Alt+R (READ01).
- Sleeping tabs for memory reclaim (SLEEP01).
- Picture-in-Picture on videos (PIP01).
- About dialog updated; Tools menu deduplicated (ABOUT01, MENU01).
- Accessible names on every chrome control (A11Y01).
- History entries show real site favicons that persist across restarts
  (host-keyed icon store under the data dir; HIST01).
- File menu: "New Tor Window" moved up next to New Window/New Tab
  instead of sitting at the bottom (MENU02).
- The engine-switch button moved out of the URL bar's right end into
  the navigation toolbar as a fixed-width button immediately right of
  the URL bar — one shared control that mirrors the active tab's
  engine instead of a per-bar embedded glyph (ENG08).
- Switching a tab's engine no longer breaks the strip: swapping the
  chip of a collapsed tab group keeps the group collapsed around its
  next member instead of spilling all hidden tabs onto the strip, and
  the swapped tab's original slot, pin and neighbours are preserved
  (a foreign-engine tab still drops group membership — documented)
  (ENG09).
- A plain `qmake && make` can no longer silently drop the Servo
  backend: while the servo-embed artifact exists under
  `spikes/servo/servo-embed/target/`, the build auto-enables it and
  qmake announces the resolved engine set; opt out with
  `qmake CONFIG-=servo`, `noservo=1` or `servo=0` (ENG10).
- The rendering-engine dropdown in Preferences shows the real Servo
  version ("Servo 0.7.0") next to the versioned Chromium entry
  instead of "Servo (experimental)", read from the servo-embed
  library itself; artifacts built before the version export fall
  back to "Servo (version unknown)" (ENG11).
- Fixed a whole-process crash on the Servo backend: libservo permits
  exactly one web view per process lifetime — a second engine tab
  (re-swapping a tab back to Servo, a second Servo tab, or reload after
  the swap) tripped an unrecoverable panic inside the engine that
  aborted the browser. The backend now allows a single Servo view and
  refuses further engine tabs with a status-bar message instead of
  crashing; sibling actions on an empty Servo tab are no-ops (ENG12).
- Servo backend hardening: engine callbacks for url, title, load status
  and frames now run on the GUI thread and are ignored once the Servo
  view is being torn down, closing a use-after-free window during tab
  close (AUDIT02).
- The URL bar works on Servo tabs: it was a read-only echo, so typing
  an address or a search and pressing Enter did nothing. Engine tabs
  now get the same editable omnibox as every other tab — shared
  history/search completion included — and Enter navigates that tab's
  own engine page instead of dying on a WebEngine-only dispatch path
  (ENG14).
- Fixed a doubled page icon on Servo tabs: the engine-swap path set
  the tab-strip icon AND painted the same default glyph into the
  favicon label, so every foreign-engine tab showed the standard icon
  twice. Engine tabs now use the single favicon slot every other tab
  uses, and the icon is restored when a Servo tab returns from a
  filtered container level (ENG15).
- Fixed dead reload on Servo tabs: the tab-strip reload slot and
  "Reload All Tabs" resolved through the WebEngine view, which does
  not exist on foreign-engine tabs, so both silently did nothing, and
  the menu/toolbar Reload entry never bound to the engine page at
  all. Reload now dispatches to the foreign page from every
  user-facing path, servo-embed gains a se_reload export that reloads
  without growing the session history (older artifacts fall back to a
  same-URL reload), and chrome actions a foreign engine does not map
  read disabled instead of inheriting the previous tab's state
  (ENG16).
- Fixed the start page showing "Error loading page" on Servo tabs:
  qrc: is a Qt resource scheme libservo cannot fetch, so any tab
  carrying a qrc: url — most visibly a swapped start-page tab —
  rendered Servo's own error page. The servo-embed shim's
  load_web_resource hook now consults a host resource resolver that
  answers qrc: loads straight out of the Qt resource system (document
  and subresources alike — css/js/images keep working, missing
  resources get a real 404), and engine tabs inject a small page-side
  "arora" bridge shim so the start page keeps its translated chrome
  text and working search box despite having no QWebChannel. The
  Tor-branded and Servo-branded start page variants and the bundled
  error pages load through the same path (ENG18).
- Private browsing is per-tab: File → "New Private Tab" (Ctrl+Shift+N)
  opens an off-the-record tab beside normal ones — mask-badged tab
  icon, no history/cookies/icons/session/recently-closed writes, and
  child tabs inherit the private context (PTAB01).
- Link context menu gains "Open in New Private Tab", "Open in New
  Private Window" and "Open in New Tor Window" alongside New
  Tab/Window. All five entries share one strict link gate —
  javascript:, data: and blob: links are refused everywhere — Referer
  is sent only for same-profile opens, private targets never inherit
  the page's container, and inside a Tor window the menu stays
  Tor-bound so a link can never escape to clearnet (CONT07).
- New Preferences "Performance" page: a hardware-acceleration toggle
  (--disable-gpu, restart note included), Memory Saver presets that
  drive the inactive-tab suspend clock (Never / Minimum / Balanced /
  Maximum), Lazy Load Restored Tabs — background session tabs now
  restore their title and icon without loading until first activated,
  with an "Always Load Pinned Tabs" opt-out — a positive "Prefetch
  Resources" checkbox (replaces the Privacy page's negative one), and
  a page-scoped Reset to defaults (PREFUI04).
- Energy Saver on the Preferences Performance page: Never / Turn On at
  20% Battery or Lower / Turn On When Running on Battery / Always Save
  Energy. While saving, inactive tabs sleep after 10 minutes
  (overriding the Memory Saver pick, including Never), tab hover
  previews are disabled, and tab loading animations freeze. Battery
  state is read from sysfs with live AC/battery transitions; the page
  shows the charge level and on machines with no battery the
  battery-based options honestly degrade to "Never" (PREFUI05).
- Preferences opens as a tab instead of a modal dialog, one per
  window — re-opening focuses the existing tab, OK applies + closes,
  Cancel or closing the tab discards, and settings deep links (Search
  engines, pop-up settings, Containers, command palette) open the tab
  on the right section (PREFS01).
- Preferences icons are a uniform size: the sidebar sections, the
  icon-theme picker previews and the engine list are all pinned to
  the style's small-icon metric, so mixed theme assets no longer
  render at different heights (ICONS02).
- Preferences moved from Tools → "Options..." to the bottom of the
  File menu as "Preferences..." (Ctrl+,), matching the cross-browser
  layout (MENU03).
- The "Icons" theme picker moved from the General page to the
  Appearance page of Preferences, and each option now previews a
  strip of the set's own icons instead of a single swatch (ICONS03).
- "Show All History" (Ctrl+H) now opens as a tab inside the window
  instead of a floating dialog — re-opening focuses the existing tab,
  double-clicking an entry opens it in a new tab, and the search,
  Remove and context-menu actions work live against the shared
  history model (HIST02).
- Download rows expand into a detail card (chevron on each item):
  source URL, destination path, started/finished times, total size,
  live speed and a speed-over-time sparkline, plus "Restart" and
  "Show in File Manager" actions — Restart works on completed
  downloads too and re-downloads to a fresh "-N" name (DOWN01).
- The separate Downloads window is gone — downloads live in the
  sidebar's Downloads panel (Ctrl+Y / Tools → Downloads focuses it),
  with a filter field, Date/Name/Size sorting, compact icon+size rows,
  Open and Open Location item actions, and the selected download's
  detail card in a bottom pane; download preferences moved to a
  dedicated Preferences → Downloads page (DOWN02).
- A running download now opens its detail card on its own — the card
  (source, destination, times, size, live speed graph, actions) is
  visible during the transfer without clicking the chevron, and
  collapses again when the download reaches a terminal state. A
  manual collapse mid-download or a manual expand after finish still
  wins over the automatic behavior, and a retried download expands
  again; starting a download also selects and scrolls to its row in
  the sidebar so the live card stays in view (DLUX01).
- The download detail card is restyled to match the compact row's
  polish: the duplicated filename/size header is gone, the card sits
  on a rounded theme-palette background with uniform padding, field
  captions (Source, Destination, Started, Finished, Size, Speed)
  render muted beside full-contrast values, timestamps and size/speed
  share single rows, and all actions live on one compact button row
  — readable in both light and dark themes (DLUX02).
- The download detail card no longer goes stale across a Day/Night
  theme switch: its fill is resolved from the live application
  palette and re-applied when the theme flips, so a card created
  under one theme can no longer keep the other theme's dark box with
  unreadable text. The sidebar detail pane also keeps the card's
  full height — the scrolling list above absorbs space deficits
  instead of clipping the card's rows (DLUX03).
- The expanded download details no longer sit inside a tinted box:
  the info block keeps the card's own surface with only a hairline
  separator and flush margins, so there is no color seam or wide
  frame around the fields in either theme (DLUX04).
- "Show in File Manager" / "Open Location" on a download now opens
  the file manager with the file selected — via the desktop's
  org.freedesktop.FileManager1 D-Bus interface on Linux/BSD,
  Explorer's /select on Windows, and Finder reveal on macOS — with a
  plain folder-open fallback where file selection isn't supported.
  If the download was deleted or moved, the nearest folder that still
  exists opens instead, and the action disables itself when nothing
  on the path survives (DOWN03).
- The Downloads sidebar gains a compact per-download action row —
  pause/resume, retry, remove-from-list, show-in-folder and a
  details (speed graph) toggle — acting on the selected row with the
  same per-state enablement as the card's own buttons, and the hosted
  card stays expanded while it is the pane's only content (DOWN04).
- The sidebar stays usable at its narrowest dock width: the download
  card's texts elide instead of demanding full width, its icon moves
  inline into the name row, and the bookmark/download action rows
  wrap onto extra lines instead of clipping (DOWN04).
- Preferences layout normalized across all ten pages: label/field
  pairs sit on shared grid columns, "Select…"-style buttons and combo
  boxes are capped at content width instead of stretching, group
  boxes no longer balloon to fill empty space, and each page's
  content packs to the top with consistent row spacing (PREFUI01).
- Preferences density pass: identical padding on every page and inside
  every group box (no more dead band under group titles), and the
  OK/Cancel buttons now sit in a slim right-aligned footer strip
  separated by a rule line instead of stretching across the window
  (PREFUI02).
- Preferences search: a filter field at the top of the section sidebar
  narrows the page list as you type — it matches page titles and the
  labels inside each page ("font" finds Appearance, "cookie" finds
  Privacy). Clicking or pressing Enter opens the top hit, and
  clearing the field restores the full list (PREFUI03).
- Theme selector on Preferences' Appearance page — System default /
  Light / Dark applies live without a restart, and Arora's own error
  pages, directory listings and start page follow the app palette in
  dark mode (THEME01).
- The window theme selector (now labeled "Window theme (Day/Night)")
  previews instantly: picking Light or Dark flips the application
  theme as you select it, changing the combo back or closing
  Preferences without OK restores the previous theme, and OK commits
  it (THEME02).
- Reader Mode toggle in the navigation toolbar, right after the
  reload/stop button — it appears only on article-like pages, shows
  a filled icon while the reader overlay is up, and stays in sync
  with View → Reader Mode (Ctrl+Alt+R) (READ02).
- Removed the duplicated reader-mode button embedded at the right end
  of the URL bar — the navigation toolbar toggle is now the single
  reader control on the chrome row, still tracking the active tab
  (READ03).
- The Reader Mode button is now always present on web tabs instead of
  disappearing whenever the article heuristic misses — the option to
  try reader mode is never taken away. Pages judged non-article still
  allow the toggle and report "Reader view is not available on this
  page."; article-like pages get an accent-dot "suggested" highlight
  instead. The control hides only where reader mode can never apply
  (History/Preferences-style tabs). Article detection also got more
  forgiving: the probe retries briefly while a page hydrates, re-runs
  on client-side SPA navigations (e.g. telex.hu), and accepts
  short-lead paragraphs and article/main containers carrying
  substantial copy (READ04).
- Reader Mode no longer shows a title-only overlay: article text
  wrapped in a form element renders in full instead of being stripped
  with the form, extractions that yield almost no readable text show
  an honest "Reader view is not available" status-bar message instead
  of an empty column, and embed-heavy pages no longer stall the
  reader (READ03).
- Reader Mode hardened without changing how it looks or works:
  article extraction now runs in an isolated script context the page
  cannot tamper with, the article markup is re-filtered against a
  safe allowlist before display, and the content renders inside a
  script-free sandboxed frame — scripts, event handlers, and
  javascript: links hidden in article markup are inert (READ05).
- Reader Mode reliability pass on the Rust pipeline: article text
  wrapped in a form or noscript element is no longer stripped with
  the container (the Rust sanitizer now unwraps transparent wrappers
  like the C++ one does), degenerate heading-only extractions are
  refused with an honest status-bar message instead of opening an
  empty overlay, and a wedged DOM serialization can no longer park
  the reader in a permanent busy state. Every silent dead-end in the
  probe → toggle → extract → overlay chain now logs a diagnostic
  line (READ06).
- Downloads moved from the Window menu to the Tools menu — the
  Ctrl+Y shortcut and icon are unchanged, and the Window menu now
  holds only tab/window-switching entries (MENU04).
- Preferences → Downloads gains a download-engine selector —
  "Built-in (Chromium)" or "Accelerated (Rust)", greyed out in builds
  without the rustdl backend — plus a connections-per-download field
  and a cleanup policy for the finished-downloads list (keep, clear
  on exit, or remove each entry as it completes) (DLACC01).
- Downloads on the built-in engine are fetched in parallel segments:
  Chromium's ParallelDownloading feature splits range-capable
  downloads into ~3 concurrent byte-range requests.  On by default;
  the Preferences → Downloads "Parallel segments" checkbox toggles it
  (applies at restart, works in Tor windows too) (DLACC02).
- The Accelerated (Rust) download engine is now the default in
  rustdl builds — fresh profiles route http(s) downloads through the
  segmented reqwest fetcher.  A saved "Built-in" pick is honored,
  and transfers behind an application proxy (including every Tor
  window) always stay on the built-in engine (DLACC06).
- Sidebar reworked Vivaldi-style: the panel picker is now a slim
  icon-only vertical rail instead of horizontal tabs eating the
  dock's height, and a new Preferences → Appearance → Sidebar
  section holds the enable toggle, Left/Right placement and a
  checkable per-panel list (Bookmarks/History/Downloads/Notes —
  hidden panels are never built, toggles apply live) (SIDE02).
- View menu gained a "Development Tools" submenu holding the engine
  tools: "Chromium Dev Tool" opens the inspector on the current page
  (the Tools menu's "Web Inspector" entry moved here) and "BiDi Dev
  Tool" is a visible placeholder for the upcoming BiDi backend
  (DEVT01 — relocated under Tools by DEVT04).
- "Development Tools" moved to the Tools menu (between the
  credential-store entry and User Agent), and the page context menu
  no longer offers "Inspect Element" — the inspector is reachable
  from Tools → Development Tools → Chromium Dev Tool (DEVT04).
- Tab bar position in Preferences is now a Vivaldi-style row of
  clickable preview tiles — each sketches a miniature window with
  the tab strip on its Top/Left/Right/Bottom edge, the selection
  carries a highlighted frame and check badge, and the picker works
  by mouse or keyboard (arrows move, Enter/Space commits) (TABS03).
- Status bar gains per-tab memory and live bandwidth readouts
  (Linux): "MEM 238 MB" polls the current tab's renderer RSS from
  /proc every 2s and follows tab switches and renderer crashes,
  while a network indicator shows down/up rates only while traffic
  moves (auto-hides when idle; exact download bytes are used while
  a file downloads) (SBAR01).
- Tools menu no longer carries "Ad Block..." — filter management
  moved to Preferences → Privacy → Content Blocking → "Manage
  content-blocking filters..." (the site shield's Ad Block
  Settings button is unchanged) (MENU05).
- Fixed a memory leak when a tab or window was closed while a
  print job was still running (UIAUDIT01).
- Internal cleanup: removed dead code left over from earlier UI
  work — unused accessors and a signal nothing listens to
  (UIAUDIT02).
- Internal cleanup: the Tab-bar-position and Sidebar-position
  preview pickers now share one implementation, and the tab strip
  does slightly less work per repaint (UIAUDIT03).
- Saved addresses on the Preferences → AutoFill page: a list of
  stored addresses with Add/Edit/Remove/Remove All — the editor
  collects country (full territory list, defaults to the system
  pick), name, organization, a multi-line street address,
  city/state/ZIP and phone/email, and saves once a name or street
  is entered. Addresses persist in the sealed autofill store, and
  Clear Private Data gains an "AutoFill Form Data" item that wipes
  them along with saved form fields (ADDR01).
- Saved addresses now fill web forms: fields are recognized by
  their HTML autocomplete tokens first (name, street-address,
  address-line1-3, city, state, postal-code, phone, email,
  country…) with common name/id heuristics as fallback, and the
  most recently used address fills text fields and country
  dropdowns. Submitting a filled or hand-typed address-shaped form
  offers to save it through the address editor, pre-filled with
  what was entered. Private browsing pages are never filled and
  never offered the save prompt (ADDR02).
- Preferences → Tab Settings gains "Show tab previews on hover" —
  unchecking it turns the tab-strip preview card off entirely,
  applies live to every window, and drops a card that is already
  up (TPREV01).
- Fixed: the tab-strip hover preview could show a stale or
  wrong-page image for background tabs. Previews now serve the
  snapshot the tab last painted while it was current — verified
  against the tab's current page — instead of grabbing whatever
  frame the hidden view happens to offer; the image is dropped the
  moment the tab navigates, and suspended tabs keep their
  pre-sleep snapshot (TPREV02).
- Preferences layout polish: on the Appearance and General pages the
  setting labels now anchor at the left page edge instead of
  right-aligning inside a wide column that read as center-floating,
  and the font-name fields and theme note left-align their text
  (PREFUI06).
- Preferences text fields stop at sensible widths instead of
  stretching to the window: credentials, engine names and shortcut
  nicknames cap at a short measure, the proxy host slightly wider,
  and URL/path fields (home page, style sheet, download locations,
  search engine URLs, DNS-over-HTTPS template) at a generous one;
  Browse/Choose buttons stay right beside their fields (PREFUI07).
- Sidebar position in Preferences is now a pair of clickable preview
  tiles like the tab-bar picker — each sketches a miniature window
  with the side panel docked on its Left/Right edge, with highlight
  frame and check badge on the selection, and mouse or keyboard
  operation (SIDE03).
- Bookmarks export gains a Netscape HTML format: the export dialog
  offers "Netscape bookmarks (*.html)" alongside XBEL and writes the
  standard bookmark file every browser imports — folders,
  separators, descriptions and the bookmarks-bar marker included —
  and the file re-imports cleanly through Arora's own HTML importer
  (BKEX01).
- Preferences section sidebar is easier to read: the page list's text
  is ~30% larger with icons scaled to match (kept uniform across icon
  themes and centered on the taller row), the column width re-measured
  for the longer labels, and the Appearance page's Panels checklist
  gets the same sizing (PREFUI08).
- Preferences → General gains an inline Language selector — System
  default, every installed translation, and an English fallback —
  applying immediately so the whole window retranslates live, no
  restart needed (LANG01).
- Preferences → Privacy group alignment fixed: the Cookies group's
  label/combo/button rows anchored to the left like other pages
  instead of floating right past the flush-left checkboxes, and the
  Site Permissions and Saved Passwords groups now fill the group
  width with their buttons packed to the left rather than rendering
  as a narrow centered band (PREFUI09).
- Fixed a crash: "Show All Bookmarks" or "Add Bookmark" while a
  non-page tab (Preferences, History) was current dereferenced the
  missing web view. "Show All Bookmarks" now raises the sidebar's
  Bookmarks panel — its context menu gained the dialog's manage
  actions (New Folder, Edit Name, Edit Address, Delete) — "Add
  Bookmark" opens with empty fields, and opening a bookmark or
  history entry from such a tab lands in a new tab (SIDE07).
- Preferences → Appearance font and language rows are bounded again:
  the standard and fixed-width font fields cap at a sensible width
  with their Select… buttons right beside them instead of stretching
  the page and stranding the buttons at the far edge, and the
  preferred-languages row now shows the stored list in a matching
  field (PREFUI10).
- Sidebar polish: bookmark, folder and history names display in
  full — the hidden Address column no longer claims the panel's
  width (names elide only at the true panel edge and the URL moved
  to a hover tooltip; Edit Address still works), and the icon rail
  hugs the window's outer edge, flipping sides when the sidebar is
  docked on the right (SIDE06).
- "Show tab previews on hover" moved from Preferences → Appearance
  to Preferences → Tab Settings, with the other tab-behavior
  options (PREFUI11).
- Toolbar navigation icons are one family now: the reload glyph in
  every bundled icon set is a weight-matched circular arrow instead
  of a heavier filled composite, and stop renders as a plain X
  (Breeze's stop used to be a red circled minus, Tabler's a heavy
  boxed X). On the Tor window's violet bar the nav and sidebar
  icons switch to the sets' light-glyph variants so they stay
  readable — and consistent — against the dark chrome (ICONS04).
- Sidebar and tab strip are mouse-resizable for real: the sidebar's
  inner edge is a visible grip handle (drag to resize, double-click
  to reset, the width is remembered and follows left/right docking);
  the same edge-drag works on the tab strip — width on Left/Right
  bars, height on Top/Bottom bars, with a double-click restoring
  Automatic (RSZ02).
- Icon-only tab bar mode: a checkable "Icon-Only Tabs" entry in the
  tab context menu and the View menu collapses every tab to its
  favicon — titles stay on the tooltips, new tabs inherit the mode,
  and the setting persists and applies to all windows. Collapsed
  group chips keep their label, the two-level container strip is
  unaffected, and a Left/Right strip shrinks to a slim icon rail
  (TABS09).
- Compact vertical tab strip: Left/Right tab bars now default to a
  slim ~140px rail (favicon plus a short elided title) instead of
  180px. A saved width that still holds the old default migrates to
  the new one, while a width you dragged to yourself is kept — and
  the edge drag and double-click reset work as before (TABS10).
- Vertical tab rows are compact again: on a Left/Right strip the
  selected row painted a ghost block several times its own height
  below itself (a Qt 6.12 style quirk that only shows on a tall
  strip), making the row look oversized. The draw is now clipped to
  the row's real height, matching the horizontal strip (TABS11).
- Dark theme readability: toolbar, menu, tab-strip, location-bar,
  status-bar and Preferences sidebar icons now switch to the bundled
  light-glyph variants when the app theme is Dark — previously the
  default "Native" icon set kept its dark glyphs on the dark chrome,
  making buttons unreadable. A desktop icon theme that already ships
  light-on-dark art is kept as-is, and flipping Day/Night in
  Preferences swaps the icon art live (ICONS05).
- The reader-mode button now matches its nav-bar siblings under the
  dark theme: its glyph is painted at the size the toolbar asks for
  with the palette's own button/disabled colors, instead of a fixed
  16-px bitmap that the bar upscaled into a grey blur and ghosted
  bright when dimmed. Active reader mode keeps its accent mark, and
  a Day/Night flip still re-themes it live (TRUST21).
- Tor circuit dropdown stays put: opening it refreshed the menu on
  every daemon reply and each reply kicked another query, so the
  popup re-laid itself out in an endless loop and crept sideways on
  some desktops. The open menu now refreshes without re-querying and
  only rebuilds when the circuit rows actually change (TOR13).
- Vivaldi-style application menu: the navigation bar now leads with an
  Arora logo + "Arora" button whose dropdown holds File, Edit, View,
  History, Bookmarks, Window, Tools and Help — the classic menubar is
  hidden by default on every window type, and View → Show Menu Bar
  (Ctrl+M) brings it back whenever wanted (MENU06).
- The Arora app-menu button reads bigger: its logo now renders larger
  than the surrounding navigation glyphs on every window type
  (standard, private, container, Tor) — drawn from the bundled vector
  artwork so it stays crisp at any scale, while the "Arora" label
  keeps the toolbar font (MENU07).
- Preferences → Privacy: the Cookies section's right-aligned labels
  floated the accept/keep/session rows off the group's left edge and
  looked ragged next to the checkbox rows; labels are now left-aligned
  so every row shares the page's left margin like the sibling groups
  (PREFUI12).
- BiDi Dev Tools → Network tab rebuilt on the Chrome DevTools layout:
  a toolbar row (Filter field with Invert, Preserve log, Disable
  cache, Clear), a row of type-filter chips (All, Fetch/XHR, Doc, CSS,
  JS, Font, Img, Media, Manifest, Socket, Wasm, Other), and a
  sortable table — Name, Status, Type, Initiator, Size, Time plus a
  Waterfall column whose bars are scaled real request timings. Failed
  and 4xx/5xx rows tint red, rows stay compact with no grid lines,
  "Preserve log" keeps the list across navigations, and "Disable
  cache" is a real browser-wide toggle the engine honors for every
  tab, including ones opened later (DEVT06).
- BiDi Dev Tools → Network tab now reads like Chrome's at realistic
  widths: the Name column stretches while Size, Time and Waterfall
  keep minimum widths (and a horizontal scrollbar at extreme widths)
  instead of being squeezed invisible, the Size column labels
  cache-served responses "(memory cache)"/"(disk cache)" rather than
  showing misleading byte counts, and a Chrome-style summary footer
  reports the filtered request count, bytes transferred, Finish time
  and the page's DOMContentLoaded/Load milestones. Waterfall bars
  paint for every completed request and split into waiting/download
  segments when the engine reports time-to-first-byte (DEVT07).
- A crashed Servo engine tab is no longer dead for the rest of the
  session: the crash notice now offers "Try Servo Again", which
  tears the dead web view down and builds a fresh one on the
  still-running engine — switching the tab to Chromium and back
  recovers the same way — so one engine crash no longer disables
  Servo until the whole browser is restarted (ENG24).
- The start page no longer shows faint horizontal bands: its
  background gradient was painted in repeating content-height stripes
  — invisible in the light theme, banded in the dark theme — and now
  covers the window as one continuous surface on all four start pages
  (standard, private, Tor, Servo) (SPAGE01).
- Home button on the navigation toolbar, right after the reload/stop
  button — clicking it loads your configured home page (the Arora
  start page by default) in the current tab, same as History → Home
  or Ctrl+Shift+H (HOME01).
- Preferences → Appearance → Sidebar: the "Panels" checklist now
  shows all its entries without scrolling and only takes the width
  its rows need — it used to render as a cramped box with a
  scrollbar while stretching the full page width (SIDE08).
- Preferences → Appearance → Sidebar: ▲▼ buttons next to the
  "Panels" checklist reorder the sidebar rail, and the order is
  remembered across restarts.  The section also gains working
  options: show/hide the panel toggle and close buttons,
  auto-close the panel when it loses focus, folder counters on
  bookmark folders, and lazy loading of panel contents (SIDE09).
- Preferences → Appearance → Sidebar: the "Panels" checklist and its
  ▲▼ reorder buttons now sit left-aligned under the label, and the
  buttons render as real framed arrows — they used to float
  mid-right as faint, nearly invisible marks (SIDE10).
- Rename any tab: double-click it (or use the context menu's
  "Rename Tab...") and type a new title inline — Enter commits,
  Escape cancels, and an empty name restores the live page title.
  The custom title wins over the page's own title on the strip, the
  tooltip, the window title and the hover preview, survives pinning,
  tab groups, container moves and undo-close, and is restored with
  the session (TABS12).
- Tabs move between windows for real: drag a tab off the strip and
  release it anywhere else and it opens as a new window with the
  live page — history, scroll, in-flight loads, pinned/group/
  container state and any custom title all come along. Dragging a
  tab onto another window's tab strip inserts it at that spot
  instead of loading its URL over the hovered tab. Dropping a tab
  onto another application still hands it a link, private tabs
  refuse to merge into non-private windows, and Tor windows never
  cross-merge (TABS13).
- Tor window nav buttons are readable now: back/forward/reload/stop/
  home, the sidebar toggle and the status-bar zoom buttons are
  painted as flat light glyphs on the violet bar — the same tone as
  the reader and onion buttons — and a disabled button shows a
  deliberate dimmer tint instead of a pale ghost. The app-menu logo
  keeps its colors with a subtle light backing disc so the bear no
  longer fades into the violet, and its dropdown arrow is light too.
  Non-Tor windows are unchanged (ICONS06).
- Pinned tabs keep their title: pinning used to collapse the tab to a
  bare favicon tile; now a pinned tab shows its favicon and title like
  any other tab (renames included) on both horizontal and vertical
  strips, shrinking only toward a favicon-plus-short-title minimum
  when the strip overflows — never back to icon-only (TABS14).
- Vivaldi-style '+' new-tab button on the tab strip in every
  orientation: it sits right after the last tab on Top/Bottom bars
  (instead of the far-edge corner widget) and rides as an appended row
  under the last tab on Left/Right rails, where the corner widget
  never rendered at all.  It stays visible when the strip overflows,
  tucked just inside the scroll buttons (TABS15).
- Vertical tab rail widened again: Left/Right strips now default to
  ~180px so typical tab titles show ~18-22 characters before eliding
  — the TABS10 compact ~140px default clipped them at ~10-12.  Saved
  widths that still hold a past default migrate forward, a width you
  dragged to yourself is kept, and the edge drag and double-click
  reset work as before (TABS16).
- Vertical tab rows no longer clip their titles: rows can never
  render shorter than a full text line plus the standard padding (a
  too-small saved strip height now clamps up instead of cropping the
  title), and a grouped row's title centers below the group-name pill
  instead of landing on the row's clipped bottom edge — favicons and
  close buttons ride the same band.  Both Left and Right rails, and
  Tor-violet and dark themes, render the full title (TABS17).
- The vertical rail's truncated 'History' row is fixed for real this
  time: it was never the row's height — the in-strip '+' new-tab
  button clamped itself on top of whichever row the rail was scrolled
  to, covering its label.  When an overflowing Left/Right strip
  leaves the '+' no free space it now moves inside the scroll-button
  band instead — the stacked scroll arrows share one row side by side
  and the '+' takes the row below — and on a rail that is exactly
  full with no scroll chrome it hides rather than cover a tab (TABS18).
- Bigger text in the Arora app menu: the dropdown and its whole
  submenu tree — File, Edit, View, History, Bookmarks, Window, Tools,
  Help, including nested flyouts and the dynamic History/Bookmarks
  entries — now render at ~120% of the platform menu font on every
  window type (standard, private, Tor). If you bring back the classic
  menubar it shows the same enlarged items (MENU08).
- Every static row in the application menus now carries an icon — the
  File/Edit/View/History/Bookmarks/Window/Tools/Help entries plus the
  static rows inside the container and DevTools flyouts, so the icon
  column reads uniform instead of ragged. 19 new mid-grey glyphs in
  the bundled fallback set cover the freedesktop-name gaps (panel
  toggles, import/export, private mask, Tor onion, reader, PiP, page
  source, terminal); native icon themes substitute their own artwork
  where the names are standard. Separators and the dynamic rows
  (history/bookmark entries, window list, encodings, user agents)
  stay iconless by design (ICONS07).
- Enabled extensions finally get a toolbar face: every loaded
  extension that declares an action popup shows its own manifest icon
  in a strip right after the address bar — Bitwarden's shield
  included — and clicking it opens the extension's real popup UI in a
  small anchored bubble that sizes itself to the page, never a new
  tab. Turning an extension off or unloading it removes the button
  immediately; extensions without popups stay out of the bar, and
  private or Tor windows never show the strip (EXT08).
- The Preferences > Extensions list now shows each extension's
  version next to its name — read from the extension's own manifest,
  so Bitwarden shows "2026.9.3" while built-in components with no
  manifest on disk show an honest "—". When an update check finds a
  newer package the cell reads "1.0.0 → 2.0.0 available" (EXT09).
- The Arora app-menu logo is now actually bigger: the previous bump
  only set a property Qt ignores for toolbar-hosted buttons, so the
  glyph still painted at the shared nav-icon size. The button now
  renders its bear at 36px — twice the navigation glyphs and clearly
  dominant over the "Arora" label — on every window type including
  Tor, where the light backing disc grew to match (MENU09).
- Menu flyout headers that sit among iconed rows now carry an icon
  too: File → "New Container Tab" shows a crate glyph, Tools →
  "Development Tools" code brackets, View → "Text Encoding" a
  letterform, Tools → "User Agent" a person bust, and History →
  "Recently Closed Tabs" a restore-windows glyph — so no static row
  reads naked in the icon column. Standard freedesktop names let
  native themes substitute their own artwork; five new mid-grey
  glyphs cover the bundled fallback set (ICONS08).
- The app-menu Arora logo can no longer paint stretched: the
  bear-globe mark now renders into a centered square of whatever
  space the widget style offers, so it stays round at every scale —
  including fractional display factors and the Tor window's styled
  bar, where the light backing disc stays a perfect circle too. The
  rest of the navigation row also centers against the taller app
  button instead of riding the top edge, so the bar reads as one
  intentional row (MENU10).
- The navigation bar is customizable: right-click it for a menu with
  New Tab and New Private Tab up top plus a checkable "Edit
  Navigation Bar" — while it's on, the bar shows a dashed frame and
  every item (app menu, history buttons, reader/onion buttons, the
  address bar itself, the sidebar toggle) can be dragged to a new
  spot behind a highlighted drop line. Exit by toggling the entry
  again, pressing Esc, or clicking anywhere off the bar. The dragged
  order persists across restarts and is shared live by every open
  and new window — normal, private and tor alike (tor keeps its
  violet chrome) — and the menu's "Reset Navigation Bar" restores
  the stock layout (NAVB03, NAVB04).
- The Preferences > Extensions table no longer wastes its width on
  the Source column: Version, Status and Source now hug their short
  fixed strings (Source stays user-resizable at a compact width)
  while the long free-text column takes the leftover space, so the
  description sits immediately right of Source instead of stranded
  at the far right (EXT10).
- Every chrome icon now shares one house style: menus, the navigation
  bar, tab-strip controls, the sidebar, location-bar indicators,
  dialogs and the Preferences lists all paint the themed glyph in a
  single palette-tinted monochrome — no more colorful artwork mixed
  among grey peers — and the tint follows light/dark flips and icon-
  theme changes automatically. Brand and page icons deliberately keep
  their own colors: the Arora and Qt logos, container-color chips,
  favicons and site/search icons, extension artwork, and the private-
  browsing badge; the Tor window keeps its light-on-violet glyphs
  (ICONS09).
- The docked sidebar no longer shows two close buttons side by side:
  the dock title bar's own X used to sit next to the panel's styled
  close button, so it stays hidden while docked — the Downloads
  header X and the rail's fold button are the single close path.
  Tear the dock off into a floating window and the title-bar X comes
  back, still governed by Preferences' "Show panel close button"
  option (SIDE11).
- Preferences → General homepage and startup reworked: the homepage
  is now a Start Page / Specific Page pair (Set to Current Page still
  fills in the address, and the Home button plus startup-homepage
  honor the choice), "Startup with" offers Last Session, Homepage,
  Start Page or Specific Pages — the last takes a list of addresses,
  one per line, each opening in its own tab. A new Close and Exit
  section hosts the moved "Show Close Window Confirmation Dialog"
  plus a new "Show Exit Confirmation Dialog" that prompts before the
  whole app quits. Saved preferences from older versions migrate
  automatically (PREF04).
- "Lazy Load Restored Tabs" moved to Preferences → General under
  "Startup with", next to the session-restore setting it modifies —
  the "Always Load Pinned Tabs" opt-out moved with it and now sits
  properly indented beneath its parent checkbox. Background session
  tabs still restore their title and icon without loading until
  first activated; saved picks carry over (PREF05).
- The Arora app menu now has a direct "Preferences..." entry between
  Tools and Help — the same item as File > Preferences, so the
  shortcut and behavior are identical — on every window type,
  including Tor windows where the menu button is the only menu
  (MENU11).
- The duplicate File > "Preferences..." entry is removed — the app
  menu's direct entry is the single menu home and Ctrl+, still
  opens the settings tab (MENU12).
- The Arora app-menu button is icon-only now — the "Arora" wordmark
  moved off the button to a quiet label at the right end of the
  navigation bar, on normal, private and Tor windows. It is fixed
  chrome: the edit-mode drag reorder cannot pick it up or drop
  items past it (MENU13).
- File > "Open Location..." is removed — the omnibox is always on
  the navigation bar, and Ctrl+L / Alt+D / Alt+O still focus and
  select it (MENU14).
- "Quit" moved out of the File menu — it is now the last entry in
  the Arora app-menu dropdown, below Help, on every window type.
  Ctrl+Q is unchanged (MENU15).
- The "Arora" wordmark is gone from the right end of the navigation
  bar, and the app-menu logo button on the left now renders at the
  same icon size as the other navigation buttons instead of
  oversized — the bar's right end simply ends at the last real
  control (MENU16).
- The app-menu logo button is now a deliberate brand accent: the
  Arora mark paints ~25% larger than the other navigation glyphs,
  scaled uniformly with no stretching, while the button face and the
  toolbar's row height stay compact — on normal, private, and Tor
  windows alike (MENU17).
- Flatter, more modern chrome from one central style source: sunken
  toolbar/dock/panel frames and hard borders are replaced by subtle
  palette-derived hairlines, toolbars and panels share a consistent
  4/8/12px spacing rhythm, group headers render semibold, and the Tor
  window's violet styling is defined once and reused everywhere — all
  of it tracks light/dark theme changes live (THEM01).
- Tab strip restyle: squared tabs are now rounded cards — the active
  tab lifts and merges into the page, inactive tabs sit flat with
  dimmed titles, hover is a soft intermediate step, and the strip's
  buttons share a rounded hover; group bands, container chips, and
  private badges stay legible on both the horizontal strip and the
  vertical rail (THEM02).
- Dialog and panel polish: Preferences sections read as semibold
  headers over a thin palette hairline instead of sunken group frames,
  page spacing follows the shared rhythm, and the page rail is a
  frameless rounded-selection list; sidebar panels keep flat hairline
  borders and consistent headers; menus and dropdowns share flat
  padding, hairline separators, and a rounded selection — Tor windows
  follow the same rules through the palette (THEM03).
- Palette unification + contrast pass: every hardcoded color in the
  chrome now lives in the one central style source — the container
  accent set, secure-field and loading cues, the warning banner, and
  the forced dark variant join the Tor violet family — and text and
  icons on selections and accent fills pick their foreground by
  measured WCAG contrast, fixing unreadable cases like white labels
  on bright container chips (THEM04).
- Firefox-matching tab size: tabs are now ~33px tall on a ~36px
  strip (up from the compact ~26-28px band), every titled tab —
  pinned tabs included — holds the same 225px width while space
  allows, they squeeze to equal widths down to a 76px floor, and
  only then do the scroll buttons take over instead of the strip
  stretching or scrolling early.  A strip height you dragged to
  yourself still wins over the automatic size, vertical-rail rows
  adopt the same uniform padding, and when the '+' new-tab button
  parks over a full strip's last tab that tab's close button yields
  rather than sit under a dead click target (TABS19).
- The site shield is now a permanent member of the navigation bar —
  one shared button directly left of the address field (right of the
  reader-mode button) that follows the active tab instead of living
  inside each tab's location bar.  It can no longer go missing on
  fresh or session-restored windows, and on tabs without a web page
  (Preferences, history, engine tabs) it stays in place disabled with
  an honest "not available on this tab" tooltip instead of leaving a
  gap (TRUST15, placement locked in TRUST16).  The shield's popover
  also opens at the right size on the very first click now — a stale
  cached geometry could squash it (and leave stale permission-row
  buttons behind) until the second click (TRUST17).
- The Downloads sidebar's header gains a "Clear download list" button
  next to the close X: one click (after a confirm) removes every
  finished, failed or cancelled entry while in-progress downloads and
  the files on disk stay untouched; the button disables itself when
  the list is empty or only active downloads remain (DOWN05).
- The start pages no longer carry a footer: the "Search results
  provided by …" attribution and the "About Arora" link are gone from
  all four variants (standard, private, Tor, Servo) — the page is just
  the header, the search box and the brand mark (MENU18).
- The window-theme choice on Preferences → Appearance is now a row of
  clickable preview tiles — Light, Dark and Auto each sketch a small
  page in the scheme they commit (Auto paints a half-light/half-dark
  split for "follow the desktop"), the selection carries a highlighted
  frame and check badge, and mouse or keyboard (arrows move,
  Enter/Space commits) both work. The pick still previews instantly
  and persists as before (THEM05).
- The app-menu logo button grows another ~25% — the Arora mark now
  paints at ~29px against the 18px navigation glyphs (about 1.6x),
  still scaled uniformly with no stretching. The navigation bar keeps
  its exact row height: the bigger button face borrows the row's
  vertical padding instead of pushing it taller, on normal, private,
  and Tor windows alike (MENU19).
- View > Zoom Text Only finally zooms text: the checkbox used to check
  and uncheck without changing anything (Chromium has no text-zoom
  factor). With it on, zoom steps scale the page's default and
  fixed-pitch font sizes instead of the whole page — text grows or
  shrinks while images and fixed-pixel layout stay put, the change is
  per tab, and it survives reloads and navigations. Text that pins a
  pixel size in CSS does not scale — an honest Chromium limit (VIEW08).
- Diagnosed the "memory indicator always climbs on article → back"
  report: the status-bar readout is honest — it polls the real
  /proc RSS of the tab's renderer — and the climb is real renderer
  memory. A scripted 20-cycle loopback fixture (tst_memcycle) shows a
  monotonic ~5.4 MB/cycle climb on every surface including a bare
  engine control with no Arora wiring, so the retention lives in
  Chromium's per-navigation session-history handling, not in any
  Arora object; the fix pass is tracked separately (MEM05/MEM06).
- Profile picture pipeline: a qApp-owned ProfileIdentity owns the
  display name ("profile/name", default "Profile") and the custom
  avatar at <data-dir>/profile-avatar.png, emitting nameChanged /
  avatarChanged so chrome repaints live; the Firefox/Vivaldi-style
  editor dialog crops through a square viewport with a cover-fit
  zoom slider (1x-4x, wheel zoom too), clamped drag-pan and a
  circular mask preview, and saves a 128px PNG atomically.
  'Remove Picture' deletes the file and falls back to the bundled
  silhouette.  Ready-made 'Change Picture...' / 'Remove Picture'
  actions ship via ProfileAvatarActions for the profile menu to
  pick up (PROF04) (PROF02).
- Profile button on the navigation bar: a round avatar now closes the
  bar's right edge (mirroring the Arora logo button on the left) and
  drops a menu showing the profile avatar and name — "Edit Profile
  Name..." renames it and the new name shows on every open window
  right away and is kept across restarts. Until you pick a picture
  the face is a neutral silhouette that stays readable on the
  violet Tor bar too; the button is a normal navigation-bar item, so
  Edit Navigation Bar can move it like any other (PROF01).
- Multiple browser profiles: the profile button's menu gains a "New
  Window" submenu listing every profile and a "Profile Manager..."
  entry, and the same manager opens from Tools > Profiles... and
  Preferences > General.  The manager lists each profile with its
  avatar, name and data size, and can create, rename, set a picture
  for, delete and open a window on a profile — deleting asks you to
  type the profile name first, and the default profile cannot be
  deleted.  Profiles are kept under their own data folders with a
  shared registry, ready for per-profile windows once that layer
  lands; until then, opening a window on another profile is disabled
  with an explanation rather than silently falling back (MPROF05).
- Tab strips no longer draw a line under resting tabs: the thin seam
  that ran along the strip's page-facing edge (and gapped under the
  active tab, so some tabs looked underlined and some didn't) is gone
  on Top/Bottom bars, resting rows on the Left/Right rail no longer
  carry a bottom hairline, and the page content is no longer boxed by
  a frame under the strip.  Tab-to-tab separators and group/container
  accent stripes are unchanged (TABS10).
- Settings split for the coming multi-profile support: every
  persona-flavored preference — privacy toggles, homepage and
  startup pages, search-engine choice, cookie policy, downloads
  directory, tab/sidebar/UI prefs, containers, per-site grants —
  now persists to `profiles/<id>/settings.ini` under the data
  directory instead of the shared QSettings file, while app-level
  keys (Tor binary prefs, crash consent and crash sentinels, engine
  defaults, CA trust exceptions, machine-wide blocklist metadata)
  stay on the global file. The first launch copies existing values
  into the default profile's store — a copy, not a move, so the old
  file remains a harmless read fallback. Only the "default" profile
  exists today; this is the storage plumbing the profile switching
  work binds to (MPROF04).
- The app's data stores are per-profile too: history, bookmarks,
  autofill, cookie rules, adblock subscriptions, containers,
  extensions, download history, notes and the credential store each
  resolve a profile-keyed instance that reads and writes under
  `profiles/<id>/`, and page-level actions (notes, custom adblock
  rules, the site panel and shield) follow the profile of the page
  they act on. Private and Tor browsing stay volatile — nothing
  reaches any profile's store. Still no visible change: every window
  runs the default profile until window↔profile binding lands
  (MPROF02).

Search & location bar:
- Omnibox: address-shaped input navigates, the rest searches (SRCH01).
- Dedicated Search settings page with inline engine editor and
  per-context engines (SRCH02/04/05).
- Shortcut nicknames @bookmarks/@history/@tabs (SRCH06).
- Dedicated search box is opt-in and fully hidden by default — no
  residual engine button beside the location bar; Ctrl+K falls back
  to the omnibox while it is off (SRCH03, SRCH08).
- DuckDuckGo default engine (DENG01).
- Fixed: a bare term like "hup" could navigate to https://hup/ instead
  of searching; a missing or broken search engine now falls back to
  the built-in default instead of a bogus address (SRCH07).
- Fixed: pressing Enter in the search field or location bar could
  silently do nothing — a missing or broken engine produced no URL,
  and a malformed bare term could build an invalid http:// guess that
  was dropped without feedback. Search now always rescues to a working
  engine, invalid guesses search instead of dead-ending, and dropped
  loads log a warning (SRCH09).
- Fixed: Backspace in the URL or search bar could dead-stop or fight
  the completion popup. A field still showing its baseline text (just
  navigated or just focused) now clears with a single Backspace —
  matching Chrome/Firefox — and once you edit mid-text it deletes
  character-wise to empty; history completion, live search
  suggestions and a re-highlighted completer row can no longer
  re-extend the field during a delete burst (SRCH10).
- Bundled search engines now show their own icons in the Engine
  dropdown even on a fresh profile — the real favicons ship with the
  browser instead of depending on a favicon-cache hit from prior
  browsing; custom engines still resolve their icons remotely
  (SENG01).
- Four more bundled search engines: Bing, Qwant, Startpage and Ecosia
  (with icons, and search suggestions for Bing/Qwant/Ecosia).
  Descriptors can now carry default keyword nicknames via an <Alias>
  element — type "b arora" for Bing, "q arora" for Qwant, "sp arora"
  for Startpage or "e arora" for Ecosia. The defaults only claim free
  keyword slots: a nickname you bound or unbound yourself always
  wins (SENG02).
- One reload button, not two: the duplicate stop/reload button at the
  right end of the address bar is gone.  The toolbar's combined
  stop/reload button — left of the address bar — is the single
  control and still flips to Stop while a page loads; the address bar
  keeps its thin loading strip along the bottom edge as the load cue
  (NAVB02).
- Search suggestions off by default, opt-in per engine (SEC11).
- Right-clicking a text or search input offers "Add to the toolbar
  search" again — the editable-field menu item lost in the WebEngine
  port. Arora reads the enclosing form in-page and turns a GET form
  into a {searchTerms} engine template (hidden fields, checked boxes
  and select values ride along; a multiple-submit form asks which
  button carries the query, and POST forms get an honest "not
  supported" warning instead of a silent no-op) (SRCH01).
- Print and Print Preview merged into a single Print entry
  (Ctrl+P), matching Firefox: the menu item and JS window.print()
  open the print-preview dialog directly, and the preview's own
  Print action runs the native print dialog (printer selection,
  copies and page range) on the generated PDF. There is no longer a
  straight-to-printer path, and PDF document tabs preview their
  staged document the same way (PRINT02).

Security:
- HTTPS-First upgrades and HTTPS-Only interstitial; real-TLS-failure
  downgrade marking only (SAFE01, SAFE07).
- Insecure form-submission warning (SAFE02).
- Domain-emphasis + IDN punycode address bar (SAFE03).
- Prefetch and remote-font request blocking (SAFE04).
- Download file-name sanitization (SEC01).
- External-protocol launch consent (SEC02).
- Encrypted credential store + optional master passphrase (SEC03/13).
- Size-capped, DTD-free untrusted-input parsers (SEC04).
- Web permission broker for notifications/geolocation/etc. (SEC05).
- Certificate-error interstitial (SEC06).
- Private-browsing store audit (SEC07).
- WebChannel bridge hardening (SEC08).
- Chrome markup-injection fixes (SEC10).
- Clear Private Data removes DOM storage too (SEC12); the wipe now
  also reaches pages where JavaScript is blocked — the injected
  storage-clear runs through a lifted path instead of being silently
  dropped (SEC12, ENG04).
- Weak TLS cipher suites removed (TLS01).
- Per-site JavaScript control + security-level tiers (JSCTL, SECLVL).
- Referer level selector (REF01).
- Tracking ping/beacon/CSP-report blocking (PING01); Reporting-API
  report-to probe — uploads never happen on this engine, plus a
  Sec-Fetch-Dest: report tripwire in case they ever do (PING02).
- Real pop-up blocker for shaped window.open (POPUP01).
- Fingerprint-resistance countermeasures (PRIV02).
- Injected fingerprint spoofing (SAFE06) — per-page script that adds
  canvas-readout noise, masks WebGL vendor/renderer and normalizes
  navigator.hardwareConcurrency/deviceMemory/plugins; always on in Tor
  windows, per-site exemption on the shield panel. Noise, not anonymity.
  Now enabled by default (PRIV06) — a stored opt-out from before the
  flip is migrated on once, and turning it off afterwards stays off.
- Third-party-cookie and connection/storage privacy group (PRIV01).
- Zero telemetry verified by capture proxy (TELEM01) — extended to a
  forwarding capture that drives a real browsing session (navigation,
  search, download) and asserts every connection is content or
  essential; DNS prefetch is now off by default and pinned off in Tor
  windows (TELEM02).
- DNS-over-HTTPS via Qt DNS mode (DOH01).
- WebRTC/DNS leak verification (LEAK01); anonymity smoke harness
  (ANON01).
- Vanilla-Chrome user agent + matching client hints (UA01–UA03).
- browseraudit.com CSP/sandbox audits and fixes (SEC14–SEC16C).
- badssl.com 72-case matrix baseline + fix pass (BADSSL02/03).
- XS-Leak baseline and third-party WebSocket block (XSLEAK02–04).
- Adblock page-load stall fix (STALL01); the start-page hang it
  caused is now pinned by a 50-load hostile-rules regression harness
  (START01).
- WebChannel console noise eliminated (CHAN01).
- Cross-platform sandbox layer (SAND01): browsing launches re-exec
  inside a permissive bubblewrap filesystem sandbox on Linux —
  credential stores and other browsers' profile paths are masked
  (tmpfs over directories, /dev/null over files), /etc /usr /boot are
  remounted read-only, the rest of the filesystem stays writable.
  Seatbelt (macOS), AppContainer manifest (Windows) and
  unveil/pledge (OpenBSD) generators ship from the same declarative
  policy, marked untested-on-target; `arora-sandbox` is the packaged
  launcher script, `--no-sandbox`/`ARORA_NO_SANDBOX=1`/the
  sandbox/enabled setting are the escape hatches, and
  `--sandbox-status` reports the live backend and policy. The
  self-contained bundle now also ships `arora-sandbox` (SAND03).
- Sandboxed download worker (SAND02): accelerated downloads run in a
  confined `arora --download-worker` subprocess whose bubblewrap
  mount namespace grants only the download's work dir and the
  destination directory — `$HOME`, the browser profile, keys and
  cookie stores are absent entirely, the environment is scrubbed,
  and pid/ipc/uts/cgroup namespaces are unshared (the network stays
  shared so the transfer can connect). Policy checks (HTTPS-first,
  HTTPS-Only, blocklists, redirect rules) still run in the browser
  process and answer the worker over a stdin/stdout protocol, so the
  sandboxed path is policy-identical; if the wrap cannot start the
  download falls back to the in-process engine. Escape hatches:
  `ARORA_DL_NO_SANDBOX=1` or `downloadmanager/sandboxedWorker=false`.
  Seatbelt/AppContainer/unveil worker policies generate from the
  same inputs but are untested off-Linux.
- In-browser PDF viewer + sanitize-on-view pipeline (PDF01): PDFs open
  in Chromium's built-in PDFium viewer instead of falling through to
  the download path (Preferences → Privacy → PDF Documents; the viewer
  is on by default). In `CONFIG+=rustcore` builds the sanitize pass is
  also on by default: a .pdf navigation is refused, the bytes are
  fetched through the page's own profile (cookies, Tor SOCKS routing
  and container isolation ride along by construction), rewritten by a
  Rust policy pass that strips JavaScript, /Launch, auto-actions
  (/OpenAction, /AA), embedded files, XFA/AcroForm remnants and remote
  submit/import actions, and only then displayed — from a randomized
  owner-only temp file, not from the remote origin. Outbound http(s)
  links work; links to local files from the staged viewer document are
  refused. Saving a PDF still stores the original unsanitized bytes
  for the external reader — sanitization covers the viewing path only.
- Shell-owned, engine-neutral PDF document view (PDF02): PDFs now
  render in a Qt document widget owned by the shell instead of
  Chromium's embedded PDFium viewer — the same surface every engine
  backend can host, since libservo ships no viewer. Pages are
  rasterized by `arora --pdf-worker`, a sandboxed subprocess running
  the pure-Rust `hayro` renderer under a bubblewrap allowlist (staged
  PDF read-only, one writable work dir, no network namespace, no
  $HOME), with page bitmaps marshaled back over a single-purpose
  JSONL protocol. The view provides continuous scrolling, zoom, page
  navigation, find-in-document and print handoff; PDF01's sanitize
  pass still always runs before the renderer sees a byte, and
  Tor/private profiles get the identical in-browser view — no
  external handoff. Preferences → Privacy → PDF Documents gains
  "View PDFs with the sandboxed document view" (on by default);
  unchecking it keeps the previous PDFium-viewer path as a fallback.
- External-URL ingress audit (SEC23): every path an untrusted URL can
  enter a running process — positional argv, the single-instance
  socket, the QDesktopServices `http` handler and the `--tor` operand —
  now funnels through the same untrusted-source gate; `openUrl()` was
  the one bypass and is fixed. The socket control channel
  (`aroramessage://`) can never be reached by a URL-shaped payload, a
  `--tor` process provably stays outside single-instance IPC, and a
  forwarded URL lands on the private profile when private browsing is
  on. Covered by `--ingress-smoke`.
- Adversarial regression lock on link context opens: the full
  source-profile x open-target matrix, refused javascript:/data:/blob:
  schemes at both menu and slot level, Referer only on same-profile
  opens, an argv-only Tor hand-off re-gated on receipt, and zero
  history/session/reopen residue from private opens (CONT08).
- Preferences > Privacy gains a Site Trust group with a master "Show
  site trust indicator in the toolbar" switch plus per-signal toggles
  for the lookalike-domain warning and the known-threat-list check —
  all on by default; turning the master off hides the trust
  badge and skips the lookups, and each signal can be disabled
  individually, applied without a restart (TRUST06, TRUST02).
- The Site Trust group also gains an "Update Now" button with a
  last-updated label: one click refreshes both remote trust lists
  (the Tranco rank table and the threat blocklist), shows progress
  while the downloads run and reports failures honestly; the button
  is disabled with an explanation until remote list downloads are
  consented to (TRUST13).
- A small padlock inside the left end of the address bar reports the
  connection and site-trust verdict at a glance — locked green for
  https on a positively verified host, locked amber for unranked or
  still-probing https, locked red when a trust signal flags the site,
  and the same tier colors unlocked over plain http; clicking it opens
  an info-only summary (TRUST14).
- The site-privacy shield moved out of the address bar onto the
  navigation toolbar immediately left of it — one button per window
  that always shows the active tab's settings (TRUST14).
- WebChannel isolation (SEC24): the page's QWebChannel is bound to a
  dedicated isolated script world, so web content can no longer
  enumerate or call the registered bridge objects — the
  qt.webChannelTransport never reaches the page's main world. The
  only path in is a whitelisted postMessage forwarder exposing just
  `window.external.AddSearchProvider` (the historical web-compat
  surface), the gesture-gated Picture-in-Picture request bridge, and
  — on internal qrc: pages only — the start page's "arora" object.
  The autofill capture bundle moved into the channel world with the
  SEC08 report token kept as a second wall; scripted form.submit()
  capture now rides a small main-world shim that relays each call as
  a DOM event.  The reader-mode driver keeps its own isolated world
  and speaks DOM events; only its tiny event relay moved into the
  channel world.
- Extension-surface hardening pass (QUAL03): a hosted extension
  background page is now navigation-locked to its own wrapper URL and
  origin — it can no longer hand the privileged chromeBridge channel
  to foreign content by navigating or iframing away. The extension
  message relay re-verifies the "<extension-id>#…" key prefix it
  mints, so one extension cannot graft itself onto another
  extension's port channels or forge sendMessage replies, and
  chrome.tabs query/get/create/update replies now scrub url/title
  unless the extension holds the 'tabs' permission or a covering host
  permission — matching the event-payload rule. The
  picture-in-picture player escapes '<' in the page-derived video
  spec it interpolates into its trusted document, closing a
  script-element breakout, and the debug cookie jar no longer logs
  rejected cookie values.

Containers:
- Isolated container profiles (CONT01).
- Per-tab container binding + inheritance (CONT02).
- Container management page in Settings (CONT03).
- Site-to-container pinning (CONT04).
- End-to-end isolation audit (CONT05).
- Optional two-level container tab strip: when multiple containers own
  tabs, a level-1 header row (color, name, live tab counts) filters
  the tab row to the selected container; header click switches, tab
  drag onto a header rebinds, headers reorder, and the single-row
  inline-chips mode remains a setting (CONT06).
- On that two-level container strip, tab favicons no longer ride up
  into the header row above the tab — they stay vertically centered
  inside the tab card at the automatic strip height and at any height
  you dragged the strip to, across tab switches and scrolling
  (TABS20).

Tor:
- Managed tor daemon plumbing (TOR01).
- New Tor Window / `arora --tor` (TOR02).
- Tor windows drop the dedicated toolbar search box entirely — the
  omnibox covers search and Ctrl+K focuses the location bar (TOR03).
- The status bar of a Tor window shows the live circuit chain
  (guard -> middle -> exit) with the full fingerprints on hover,
  refreshed on daemon circuit events and tab switches (TOR04).
- A configured DNS-over-HTTPS mode is ignored inside a Tor window —
  names resolve remotely through the SOCKS proxy, so a local resolver
  would bypass the tunnel with the whole lookup stream (SEC20).
- Each Tor circuit hop now carries its exit-country code ("(DE)",
  "--" when unknown), resolved via the tor control port and cached
  per relay fingerprint (TOR05).
- The Tor window's header block is one coherent violet scheme: the
  menu bar, bookmarks toolbar and tab strip take the navigation bar's
  treatment instead of staying on the default palette, and the
  selected tab reads as the light "field" element like the location
  bar (TOR07).
- A small onion button next to the reader-mode button in Tor windows
  opens a dropdown of the live circuit — one row per relay hop with
  its country flag and name (relay fingerprint and OR address on
  hover), refreshed on open and on daemon circuit events (TOR08).
- Tor windows open new tabs on a Tor-branded start page: the familiar
  Arora header and working search box on a full-viewport purple
  background with the official Tor onion centered below the search
  field — all bundled locally, nothing fetched (TOR09).
- Every new-tab surface in a Tor window — Ctrl+T, File > New Tab, the
  tab-strip "+" and double-clicking empty strip space — commits that
  Tor start page instead of opening a blank "Untitled" tab, now
  verified end-to-end in a real `--tor` process (ENG17).
- Clicking the Tor circuit button no longer risks a crash when the
  tor control connection drops mid-query — the dropdown now gates on
  the live control link and shows an honest "circuit unavailable"
  state instead of stale hop rows (TOR10).
- The Tor window's title area now matches the violet chrome: since a
  native title bar can't be tinted per-window, Tor windows draw their
  own — the menu row doubles as a title bar with a Tor badge, the live
  window title, and minimize/maximize/close buttons, plus a violet
  frame border. The window still drags, edge-resizes and closes like a
  normal one; regular windows keep the native look (TOR11).
- The Tor window's drawn title bar shows the page title in readable
  white text that keeps its space on the menu row — a long title
  elides gracefully instead of collapsing to a faint sliver (TOR12).
- The Tor window's status bar joins the violet chrome — the circuit
  chain, memory/network/load/zoom indicators, the size grip and
  transient messages (like hovered links) now render light-on-violet
  instead of near-invisible dark text on purple (TOR14).
- The Tor circuit button now opens a Tor-Browser-style panel instead
  of a flat hop list: a vertical chain from "This browser" through
  each relay — flag, country and role, OR address, short fingerprint —
  to the destination site, joined by a connector line, plus a "New
  Tor circuit for this site" button (SIGNAL NEWNYM — rotates the
  middle/exit hops, guard may persist, says so underneath) and honest
  status when the daemon is gone. The chain shown is the shared
  busiest circuit, not a per-site one (TOR15).
- The Tor circuit panel always opens big enough for the full chain:
  it used to keep the small "connecting" size when the circuit data
  arrived after the click, squeezing the whole chain into a sliver
  of flags. The popup now grows to fit the circuit when it lands,
  the chain reserves room for the standard three relays, the
  connector line stays visible on every theme, and both the panel's
  "New Tor circuit" button and the nav-bar onion button got proper
  sizes (TOR16).
- Tor windows are usable by default again: the bundled tor daemon now
  launches with its own library directory on the child loader path
  (the expert-bundle binary has no RPATH, so an inherited
  LD_LIBRARY_PATH could kill it at exec), an early exit retries the
  spawn instead of giving up, and a status banner above the tabs
  shows "Connecting to the Tor network…" with bootstrap progress —
  or "Tor daemon unavailable" with the reason and a Retry button —
  instead of every page dying at a raw proxy error.  The fail-closed
  proxy is never bypassed: no page ever falls back to clearnet
  (TOR17).
- Tor-window searches and navigations now survive the daemon's
  bootstrap: an address typed or a search kicked while the banner
  still says connecting is held and dispatched the moment the SOCKS
  listener is live instead of dying at a proxy error, and a page
  that still raced cold Tor circuits right after connecting reloads
  itself once a circuit is usable — no manual reload needed.  A
  genuinely failed daemon keeps the honest error page and the
  banner's Retry (TOR18).
- The Tor circuit popover now opens at the right size on the very
  first click: the menu was measured from the panel's previous
  content before the open-time refresh ran, so the first click could
  show a clipped or mostly empty popup and only the second click
  looked right (TOR19).
- The site shield on a Tor window's navigation bar now renders in the
  chrome's light text color instead of an unreadable black outline on
  the violet bar — the glyph tint follows the button's own resolved
  palette, so it stays legible on any dark chrome the same way the
  reader and circuit buttons do, and its dimmed state still reads as
  dimmed (TRUST18).
- The first URL or search typed into a fresh Tor window no longer
  silently fails: "ready" only means the SOCKS listener answers, so
  a navigation issued in the cold-circuit stretch right after it is
  held until a stream-ridable circuit (BUILT, three hops, client
  purpose) exists — with a status-bar line announcing the wait — and
  a bounded grace still dispatches when the daemon shares no circuit
  data.  Navigations that do race the cold window and fail now get
  several warm-up retries instead of one, still stopping honestly on
  the error page once the budget is spent (TOR12).
- The Tor start page's own search — and any link click or redirect a
  page kicks off — now joins the bootstrap hold instead of slipping
  past it: page-initiated navigations never reached the location-bar
  queue, so the first search on a fresh Tor window still died at the
  cold proxy and briefly showed an error page before the retry
  cleared it.  Such navigations are now refused into the same hold,
  and while a warm-up retry is armed the tab shows a "Tor is still
  starting" pending page that resolves on its own — the error page
  only commits once the retry budget is honestly spent (TOR14).
- A warm-up retry's reload could be silently swallowed while the
  "Tor is still starting" pending page was still committing, leaving
  the tab stuck on the pending page instead of retrying — the retry
  now waits for the generated page commit to land before it
  re-dispatches, so the bounded retry chain actually runs to its
  honest end (TOR14).
- The first search on a fresh Tor window no longer fails once more:
  the bootstrap-and-circuit hold was already right — the last
  failure sat one layer lower, in the engine's proxy switch itself.
  The real SOCKS endpoint is now applied the moment the daemon
  reports its listener (during bootstrap, not at the end) and a
  throwaway warm-up request makes the network layer notice the
  switch, so a navigation released on the first usable circuit can
  no longer resolve against the dead placeholder proxy and flash an
  error page (TOR17).
- Tor window polish: the reader-mode button can no longer revert to a
  black glyph when a state change lands inside a stylesheet repolish —
  it re-bakes its icon whenever the palette/style settles, the way the
  site shield already did.  The sidebar's icon rail joins the violet
  chrome too: its strip, tab glyphs and fold button now resolve
  against the rail's own light-on-violet palette instead of the
  application's (TRUST20).
- A Servo-branded start page variant appears when the default engine
  pick is Servo: the same Arora header, search box and default
  coloring, with the official Servo logo centered under the search
  field — bundled locally like the other start pages (ENG13).
- Private tabs now open on their own start page instead of a blank
  one: the usual Arora header and working search box on a deep
  midnight-blue background with a sunglasses mark under the search
  field — bundled locally, nothing fetched.  about:home typed in a
  private tab resolves to it too; a Tor window's tabs keep the Tor
  page (PTAB02).
- Downloads sidebar detail card now fits the dock's narrowest width:
  the selected download's action buttons wrap onto extra rows instead
  of being clipped or squeezed to slivers, and long URL/path labels
  elide rather than forcing the sidebar wider.  Downloads made in
  private tabs still vanish from the list when they finish — that is
  the intended privacy behavior — while normal downloads stay until
  explicitly cleared (DOWN04).
- Stop/reload now actually flips while a page loads: the combined
  toolbar button shows Stop during a load and really stops it, and
  returns to Reload on finish or failure — Qt 6 no longer reports
  intermediate load progress, so the state is driven by the load's
  start/finish edges instead.  The location bar paints a thin loading
  strip along its bottom edge while a load is in flight (NAVB01; the
  in-bar duplicate stop/reload button was removed again — NAVB02).

AdBlock:
