Servo backend + per-tab engine switcher (ENG05):
    Arora can host a second, degraded rendering engine beside Chromium. An engine registry (src/engine/engineregistry.*) enumerates live backends: QtWebEngine is always present; the Servo backend compiles in only with `qmake servo=1` and registers only when the servo-embed artifact (spikes/servo/servo-embed, pinned libservo v0.7.0) resolves through dlopen at runtime — a missing artifact hides the option instead of offering a dead menu entry, and a default build never links the ~1 GB library. A small engine indicator in the navigation row — a fixed-width toolbar button immediately right of the URL bar (ENG08 moved it out of the field) — shows the current tab's engine as a palette-safe glyph, names it in the tooltip, tracks the active tab, and offers "Reload in Servo"/"Reload in Chromium": the swap replaces the tab's page with the same url on the other backend (history and form state do not carry — the engine is fixed at page creation).
    Honesty rules per the ENG03 verdict: Tor windows are Chromium-locked (Servo has no SOCKS5), private tabs refuse the swap (no off-the-record equivalent), and Servo's capability table reports request interception, script injection/eval results, cookie filters, downloads, devtools and per-page settings as unsupported rather than faking them — `--engine-list` prints every registered backend's surface. Preferences → Tab Settings gains a default-engine combo labeling Servo experimental; it applies to explicit new tabs only while context-bound spawns (link targets, session restore, containers) stay on WebEngine. The tst_engineswitch suite drives the whole path with a fake backend, so no servo artifact is required for `make check`.

Engine-layer test suite (ENG06):
    The engine abstraction now carries its own regression net instead of borrowing WebEngine fixtures. autotests/fakeengine.h grew from a do-nothing stub into the shared reference Engine::Backend — a pure-Qt implementation covering navigation/history/zoom/find/script eval/lifecycle/render-process-identity/profile policy+storage/downloads/view ownership, every member a stageable field so a test dials in exactly the behavior it probes; the same header now backs the adapter parity tests, the per-tab swap suite and the tab-strip engine legs. `arora --engine-smoke` scripts navigation, zoom, find and script evaluation against every registered backend and diffs the observable traces: shared-contract failures (load completion, url echo, progress reaching 100, signal ordering, event kinds, terminal url) fail the run while capability-shaped divergences (progress ladders, title phrasing, unsupported script eval) are logged, not failed — the matrix runs green over WebEngine and Servo. New switcher legs drive a mid-load swap and foreign-tab close edges by widget identity; the mid-load leg caught and fixed a real bug where the swap restarted the last committed page instead of the in-flight navigation target. The memory indicator is now verified against the process-identity contract on both the fake (no process) and a live WebEngine renderer pid.

Policy verdict memoization (PERF01):
    Repeated subresource requests no longer pay the full JSON-marshal/FFI/parse cost of a NavigationPolicy::evaluate per request. evaluate() now memoizes non-main-frame verdicts in a bounded (4096-entry) cache keyed on the entire request manifest — url, first-party url, resource type, method, privacy scope, tor flag, script tier and minimum referer level, plus a length-prefixed fingerprint of every request header so Referer- and Sec-Fetch-relevant verdicts can never alias — and stamped with a monotonic verdict-state generation. The Rust core (and the no-rust fallback) bumps that generation on every mutation that could flip a verdict: snapshot/policy reloads, downgrade marks and HTTP/blocklist allowances, urlstrip ruleset and blocklist swaps, and data-directory switches; entries stamped with an older generation are never served. Main frames stay uncached — their decisions read wall-clock downgrade TTLs and carry consume-once blocked-navigation side effects — and off-the-record/Tor scopes hash into the key so private verdicts never alias normal-scope ones. The cache never crosses into GUI state and ARORA_POLICY_CACHE_OFF=1 disables it for diagnostics; tst_navigationpolicy's verdictCache case pins the hit/miss/staleness contract and --privacy-smoke now reports requestsSeen/policyEvals/cacheHits so a repeated subresource fetch is observed skipping the backend evaluation entirely.

Engine switcher explains locked tabs (ENG19):
    The engine indicator no longer vanishes in contexts where switching is refused — a disappearance users read as a bug. In Tor windows, on private tabs, and while a container tab level is active, the nav-row button now stays visible but greyed-out, with a tooltip and status tip naming the reason ("Tor windows are locked to Chromium…", "private tabs stay on the built-in engine…", "engine switching is unavailable while a container tab level is active"); the swap itself stays unreachable as before. Widget tabs (Preferences, History) and installs with a single registered engine still hide the cell outright — there is no engine choice to explain. Dead targets are honest too: when Servo's one-tab-per-session view slot is already taken, "Reload in Servo" appears disabled with the reason instead of failing silently, and the button stays live so swapping back to Chromium always works.
Rust coverage gate (COV06):
    `make check-rustcoverage` — now also a `make check` prerequisite — measures per-crate line coverage over every shipped Rust crate (rustcore, rustdl, the ad-block engine; new crates are picked up automatically, spikes excluded) with cargo-llvm-cov and enforces an 85% minimum each. ARORA_RUST_COVERAGE_MIN overrides the gate; machines without cargo or the pinned cargo-llvm-cov tool skip cleanly, matching the optional-Rust build rules. Each run refreshes the per-crate table in .devin/COVERAGE.md; current coverage is 86.9% (rustcore), 89.3% (rustdl) and 90.5% (adblock).

Rust unit-test lift (COV07):
    rustcore's line coverage rose from 86.9% to 91.6%. The CDP-to-BiDi translator now has a loopback socket harness that exercises command dispatch, the session attach flow, event translations, websocket frame boundaries, discovery/handshake rejection paths and the FFI entry points end to end against a scripted fake devtools server, and the network-policy engine's whole call_json op surface — flags, referrer shaping, upgrade/downgrade marks, http allowances, blocked-navigation bookkeeping, the domain blocklist and the probe ops — plus its parser and URL-helper edges are under test. The snapshot loader also learned to drop whitespace-only https-only exception entries.

C++ coverage drivers + refreshed baseline (COV09):
    The make check-coverage harness now also exercises the pdf, pdfview, sigterm, servo, crash and fingerprint-inject smokes plus the write-sandbox-launcher utility, a second --pdfview-smoke pass under ARORA_BWRAP=/nonexistent covering the in-process raster fallback real bwrap-less systems take, and direct stdio JSONL drives for `arora --download-worker` and `arora --pdf-worker` — both subprocesses only ever run inside bwrap in production, where their profiles can't reach the coverage dir. New autotest legs cover the ad-block cosmetic-script payload builder, the BiDi devtools panel's JSON handlers, the model toolbar's drag/drop and context-menu paths, and the popup-blocker location-bar indicator. The regenerated src/ baseline moved from 77.97% to 83.36% line coverage.

C++ coverage: no ~0% files left (COV10):
    A new tst_torinterceptor autotest drives the tor profile's request interceptor — previously the suite's only ~0%-coverage source file at 8% lines — through every verdict arm against a throwaway off-the-record profile and a loopback fixture: clearnet http-to-https upgrades (main frame and subresource, including the script-type snapshot branch), the .onion exemption, Allow verdicts with referer rewriting, the Safer script gate, the domain blocklist beating the upgrade stage, and non-web scheme passes. The file now measures 100% lines and the regenerated src/ baseline moved from 83.36% to 83.43% — no source file with more than 100 instrumented lines reports near-zero coverage.

C++ coverage: mid-tier lifted to 85.5% (COV11):
    The src/ baseline moved from 83.43% to 85.50% line coverage, clearing the 85% gate target with drift margin. The harness now isolates each smoke leg's shared test-mode data dir — leftover ad-block subscription rules were silently neutering every later JavaScript-dependent leg, which had kept the security-level smoke's whole phase machine uncovered — and the client-certificate smoke runs against a locally minted PEM + loopback fixture instead of live badssl, with an added refused-connection download leg for the interrupted/cancelled arms. New tests cover the extension manager's permission lists, parsers, consent and update fetch (tst_extensionmanager), the PDF view's keyboard/wheel/drag, find-bar and print arms (driven inside --pdfview-smoke), the tab bar's container header-band gestures (tst_tabbar containerBandEvents), the bookmarks dialog's open/edit/new-folder/context-menu slots, the XBEL reader's error and element-mix paths, and the download graph's populated paintEvent.

C++ coverage gate now enforces 85% (COV12):
    `make check-coverage`'s ARORA_COVERAGE_MIN default is 85 (raised from the original 80 gate set when the baseline stood at 80.5%) against an honestly measured 85.50% src/ line baseline, so coverage regressions now fail the check instead of drifting. The regenerated .devin/COVERAGE.md header records the COV01->COV05/COV08 gate lineage.

Servo tabs render real page pixels again (ENG21):
    A tab swapped onto Servo showed only empty space — the pinned libservo_embed.so artifact predated the panic-containment source's se_resize fix, which routes resizes through WebView::resize so the painter's bookkeeping (document view, repaint, pipeline display list) actually runs; the stale document view left WebRender compositing nothing (and asserting on GL state under a debug artifact, which then poisoned the instance into a permanently blank tab). The rebuilt artifact renders loaded pages correctly — the start page, internal qrc pages and real files all land pixels in the tab widget — and --servo-smoke now guards the regression with a servo-renders-pixels leg that grabs the engine view and fails on a solid-color frame rather than settling for "no crash".

A Servo engine crash can no longer take the browser down (ENG20/ENG22):
    Swapping a tab onto Servo used to be able to abort the whole process — a panic inside the engine (the reported case was the painter asserting on the first resized frame) unwound across the servo-embed C ABI, which Rust forbids, so the browser died outright. Every export now funnels through a catch_unwind boundary: the first caught panic poisons the instance, every later call short-circuits to an error return instead of touching incoherent engine state, and the panic text still lands on stderr for diagnosis. On the Qt side every entry point is gated on se_is_poisoned — a crashed engine stops being pumped rather than spinning on dead state — and the tab paints an honest "The Servo engine crashed — switch it back to Chromium" notice; swapping the tab back still works (teardown of a poisoned instance is itself catch-wrapped). se_resize also routes through WebView::resize so the painter's document-view bookkeeping runs — the ordering fix behind the original abort. --servo-smoke now injects a real panic through se_spin (SE_SELFTEST_PANIC) and asserts the whole chain end to end: caught, poisoned, every later entry point a no-op, browser alive.

Servo tabs no longer crash on real desktop sessions (ENG23):
    A Servo engine tab still panicked at the painter's GL error check on a real desktop display (Wayland/X11 with a hardware adapter) — containment caught it, but the engine died. The shim rendered through a display-bound hardware EGL context even though frames only ever get read back into a QImage; stray driver errors left on that context (notably by swap-chain resizes) tripped the painter's strict error assert. Servo's painter is now pinned to Mesa's surfaceless software rasterizer (llvmpipe) — the same path every headless run already used — chosen before the display/driver is picked, with a fallback retry on the real display for non-Mesa stacks. The shim also releases foreign GL contexts it finds holding the init thread, unbinds its own context after every call so Qt's compositor isn't disturbed, and drains sticky GL errors around paint/resize/teardown so a driver hiccup is a log line instead of a dead engine. A new --servo-smoke leg verifies the software rasterizer actually engaged rather than assuming the override took.

BiDi Dev Tools Network tab gains Chrome's status line and real column floors (DEVT07):
    The request table squeezed its Size, Time and Waterfall columns to nothing at realistic dock widths — a bare stretch column grows into slack but never shrinks back, and the header's global minimum could not floor just the waterfall. Name and Waterfall now split the flex space with real 160px/140px minimums while the numeric columns stay fixed-fit, scrolling horizontally at extreme widths instead of vanishing. A Chrome-style footer below the table reads "N requests · X transferred · Y resources · Finish · DOMContentLoaded · Load" — the load timings ride the Page domain on the same monotonic clock as the request rows and reset on each navigation even when Preserve log keeps the rows. Size is honest about what is known: the response's own byte count appears as an interim value while the body loads, cache-served rows mark "(cached)" rather than a misleading "0 B", and the resources total uses decoded (uncompressed) body bytes the bridge now tallies from the transfer stream.

Devtools menu entries are plain actions again (DEVT08):
    Tools → Development Tools showed a checkable tick box on "BiDi Dev Tools" while its "Chromium Dev Tool" sibling had none — the BiDi row had borrowed the panel dock's toggleViewAction, which is checkable by design. Devtools rows are open/close commands, not persistent visibility states like the sidebar, so the entry is now a plain action that flips the dock's visibility; the dock's own close button stays authoritative and no checked state can be stranded after the panel closes.

Extension installs are integrity-pinned and update reviews show permission drift (SEC25):
    A successful install now records a SHA-256 of the extension's whole on-disk tree; a startup sweep re-hashes each installed extension and, when files changed outside the engine, flags the row "Tampered" in Settings → Extensions with a "files changed since install — review or remove" warning instead of silently loading it (uninstall clears the pin, an applied update re-pins its new files). Applying a downloaded update now diffs the new package's manifest against the installed version and lists every permission and host pattern it added as "new since installed version" — the original install consent never covers escalated grants. The review dialog also escalates broad host access visually: <all_urls> and whole-scheme wildcards like *://*/* or http://*/* carry a warning icon and a dedicated escalation row instead of reading like a normal site grant.
BiDi Dev Tools waterfall reads like Chrome's now (DEVT09):
    The Waterfall column still rendered every request as a 1–2px hairline on any real timeline — bars scaled linearly to the whole recording window, so one slow request (a hanging download, a held-open socket) stretched the axis and flattened everything else. The paint axis now zooms into the dense region: the window caps at roughly the p90 completed end whenever the true span is more than twice that dense span, while an evenly spread recording keeps the honest full axis — and the footer's Finish still reports the true end. Bars get a real 5px minimum width (sub-pixel durations stay visible, overlapping honestly like Chrome's), a darker edge keeps swatches legible on light themes, requests clipped by the zoomed window carry a right-edge overflow notch, and the page-load timings DEVT07 added now paint as column-wide DOMContentLoaded/Load marker lines. Pending rows stay translucent, failed rows stay red, and an empty or pending-only window still paints nothing phantom.

Servo wheel scrolling reaches browser pace (ENG25):
    A Servo engine tab crawled under the wheel — each notch moved the page about 14 pixels (angleDelta()/8) where Chrome and Firefox travel 150–300px, and every forwarded event paid a full-viewport software raster plus readback, so a burst of notches queued paint after paint and read as molasses. A new --servo-smoke scroll bench measured both halves first — real wheel events in, page scrollY read back, blitted frames counted — which pinned the input conversion as the main bottleneck rather than guessing at the rasterizer. Wheel notches now scale by the desktop's scroll-lines setting at ~53px per line (a notch is ~145–160px, matching other browsers), pixel-precise trackpad deltas pass through untouched, and a burst of wheel events coalesces into one scroll per frame interval instead of a repaint storm; the frame readback also reuses its image backing instead of a multi-megabyte allocation per paint. Direction, edge clamping and overscroll behavior are unchanged — the smoke asserts all of them plus the per-notch pace.

A crashed Servo engine no longer wedges the backend slot forever (ENG27):
    Tearing down a crashed Servo tab could itself panic inside webrender's GL teardown — the catch in se_destroy logged "panic during se_destroy — instance leaked" but the instance slot stayed occupied, so no later code path could ever recover: one unlucky crash meant Servo was dead for the rest of the process. se_destroy now hands the instance slot back before the fallible teardown runs — the slot holds the live pointer, so a stale or double-destroyed instance is rejected without being dereferenced — and the teardown itself runs under two panic catches so a failure in the pre-drop GL drain can't skip the drop attempt. A panicking teardown now degrades to a contained memory leak with a free slot, never a stranded slot. New exports se_instance_active/se_can_init let the host prove the slot was released and learn whether a fresh instance can be minted (the once-per-process engine still refuses a second init today — that's the next slice), and --servo-smoke injects a panic inside the teardown to assert the whole chain end to end. The deployed servo-embed artifact was also rebuilt — the pinned copy predated several engine fixes.

MV3 extension support is now measured, not guessed (EXT11):
    "If Bitwarden doesn't work, will anything?" now has a measured answer. A new --mv3-health-smoke diagnostic installs a fixture MV3 extension carrying Bitwarden's permission set onto the isolated smoke profile and interrogates it from inside its own contexts: the background runs as a real service worker and answers messages from content scripts and the popup, content scripts inject, the action popup renders, storage (local/session/managed) and runtime messaging round-trip, and alarms/idle/offscreen/i18n/observe-only webRequest are live — while chrome.extension, tabs lookup and tab events, webNavigation, scripting, contextMenus, notifications, windows, action and declarativeNetRequest are not implemented by the engine. Against the bundled Bitwarden the diagnosis is precise: its background worker dies during script evaluation on those missing namespaces (Chromium status 15, kErrorScriptEvaluateFailed) — a registered-but-unresponsive context — and its popup's Angular bootstrap throws on chrome.extension.getBackgroundPage, which is why it spins forever; the popup host itself is exonerated. No flag, setting or install option adds the missing surface (the schemas are compiled into QtWebEngineCore but its embedder never registers them), so the README now states the support boundary honestly and .devin/EXT11-report.md holds the full matrix and the EXT12 degradation plan.
A crashed Servo tab can now be restarted in place (ENG28):
    Servo used to be a one-shot engine per browser session — libservo parks its options in a process-global cell, so the embed shim fused "engine" and "web view" into a single once-per-process instance and a crash disabled Servo until Arora restarted. The two are now separate: the engine is built once and kept for the process lifetime, while each web view is just a WebView over it, so a crashed tab's view can be destroyed and a fresh one minted on the still-running engine — the same shape as Chromium respawning a renderer on the surviving browser process. The crashed tab's notice now offers "Try Servo Again", which tears down the dead objects and recreates the view in place at the last committed address (the engine-swap path recovers identically), and a new graceful se_shutdown gives the engine a clean teardown while the GUI thread is still alive — fixing a SIGSEGV that could fire at process exit. A real-display GL fault the re-init path exposed — a transient "BadAccess" on the first context bind after a generation swap that the painter only logged before drawing blind — is now absorbed and retried inside the shim instead of crashing in the rasterizer.

Extensions now report honest health instead of a fake "Enabled" (EXT12):
    Arora measures each enabled MV3 extension's background once per session — a hidden page on the extension's own chrome-extension:// origin pings its service worker and counts live background contexts — and folds that verdict together with the manifest's declared-but-missing chrome.* APIs. Settings → Extensions then shows "Limited" (worker runs, some APIs unavailable) or "Not supported" (worker never became ready) with the reason instead of a bare "Enabled", and clicking a dead extension's toolbar action opens a short explanation rather than a popup that spins forever. The bundled Bitwarden measures dead — its worker dies during evaluation on namespaces Qt WebEngine doesn't register — so it now reports "Not supported" with the missing APIs listed; extensions whose workers evaluate fine but declare unserved APIs read "Limited".

Servo tabs show an honest memory figure instead of "MEM —" (ENG26):
    The status-bar memory readout used to blank on a Servo engine tab — Servo renders in-process, so there is no per-tab renderer pid for the widget to poll and it had nothing honest to show. The engine interface now carries a resident-memory surface (a kB figure plus a "shared" flag, with the /proc VmRSS parse shared by every backend), and the Servo page reports the host process's real RSS marked as shared: the readout shows "MEM <n> (shared)" with a tooltip explaining the number covers the chrome and every other tab too — a real figure, never a fake per-tab one. WebEngine tabs still report their own renderer's RSS, and the indicator rebinds cleanly when a tab switches engines or a crashed Servo view is restarted in place.
Extensions with background scripts get a hosted background context (EXT14):
    Qt WebEngine's MV3 host cannot actually run most extension background bundles (its service-worker support is a subset and half the chrome.* surface has no embedder delegate), so enabled extensions that declare a background now get one anyway: a hidden page on the extension's own profile at its real chrome-extension:// origin runs a generated bootstrap that installs a shim preamble — error capture, service-worker globals, and a handshake over the dedicated 'chromeBridge' WebChannel — then executes the extension's real background scripts in manifest order. The installed package is never modified, so extension integrity verification still applies. The hidden context is kept answerable under Chromium's background throttling (active-lifecycle pin plus a periodic wake nudge), a crashing background can only take down its own render process and reports through the extension's health state in Settings → Extensions instead of the console, and disabling or removing the extension tears the host down cleanly. The bridge today carries the handshake and error reporting — calls into not-yet-implemented chrome.* surfaces answer a typed 'unimplemented' error that surfaces in the health row rather than silently no-op'ing; the real API surface arrives with the follow-up shim work. Set ARORA_EXTHOST_OFF=1 to disable the hosted runtime entirely.
The History sidebar can now filter by period and clear itself (HIST03):
    A compact combo in the History sidebar's header row narrows the day
    groups to Today, This Week, This Month, or All — the choice persists
    between sessions and composes with the existing search field, so
    searching "today" plus a keyword only scans today's entries.  Beside
    it a clear-history icon button asks the same confirmation the
    History menu has always shown before wiping the store.

Hosted extensions get a working chrome.* core API (EXT15):
    The EXT14 hosted background context now runs a real WebExtensions surface instead of a handshake stub: chrome.runtime (messaging, Ports, lifecycle events, getURL/getManifest/getPlatformInfo), chrome.storage.local persisted per extension plus in-memory storage.session with onChanged, chrome.extension, chrome.i18n lookups against _locales, chrome.alarms backed by host timers, and chrome.action/browserAction state. Half-implemented native namespaces are repaired rather than replaced — missing members are filled in even when Chromium re-installs its own partial objects during startup — and everything the hosted runtime cannot do answers a typed error that shows up in the extension's health diagnostics instead of silently no-op'ing. Hosted contexts of the same extension can message each other and hold Port channels through a host-side relay that never crosses extension boundaries. Real bundled webpack extensions now boot too: Bitwarden lazy-loads a JavaScript chunk through a synchronous importScripts() that no extension page can satisfy under the MV3 content-security policy, so the host delivers the manifest's background entries and their sibling chunks as one merged script injected before page scripts run — the chunk's modules register with webpack in the same pass, before its deferred require can fail. The bundled Bitwarden background boots with a 114-surface compatibility audit clean and zero unshimmed-API errors.

The Notes sidebar is now a real multi-note manager (NOTE02):
    The single scratch pad is replaced by named notes: the panel lists
    them, clicking one opens it in an editor (title field plus body),
    and header +/- buttons add or delete — deleting a note that has
    content asks first.  With zero notes the panel shows a "Write your
    first note" empty state.  Notes autosave as before and persist per
    browsing profile; a private or Tor session's notes stay in memory
    and never reach disk.  Any text already typed into the old scratch
    pad migrates into the first note automatically.

The Notes sidebar gains search, selection capture and markdown
(NOTE03):
    A search field above the notes list filters live on note titles
    and bodies.  Selecting text on a web page now offers "Add
    Selection to Notes" in the context menu — the selection becomes a
    new note titled after the page with the source URL appended.  Each
    note has a markdown flag in the editor's options menu: when set,
    the body shows rendered in place instead of source text, and the
    flag persists with the note.  The same menu's "Insert Current Page
    URL" drops the open tab's address at the cursor.
Hosted extensions now see browser events (EXT16):
    Extensions that subscribe to browser lifecycle events — chrome.tabs.onCreated/onUpdated/onActivated/onRemoved (plus onHighlighted/onMoved/onDetached/onAttached), chrome.windows.onCreated/onRemoved/onFocusChanged, chrome.webNavigation.onBeforeNavigate/onCommitted/onCompleted/onErrorOccurred (with onHistoryStateUpdated/onReferenceFragmentUpdated for same-document navigations), and chrome.contextMenus.onClicked/onShown/onHidden — now receive them in their hosted background context as the browser actually runs. A new ExtensionEventHub watches the real window set and maps engine signals to spec-shaped event objects; events reach hidden hosted contexts even while Chromium throttles their timers, and delivery follows Chrome's permission model — webNavigation and contextMenus events require the declared permission, while tab events arrive for every hosted extension but with URL and title fields scrubbed unless the manifest grants tabs or a matching host permission. Private and Tor tabs are invisible to extensions entirely. Extension-registered context-menu items now appear in the real page context menu as a per-extension submenu, filtered by their declared contexts, and clicks deliver the spec's (info, tab) arguments back to the extension.
Internal cleanup: dead-code sweep across the merged lanes (QUAL01):
    Removed unused methods, signals and members left by sequential
    task work — the never-wired Engine::Page window-creation and
    permission/context-menu request surface, dead diagnostics
    accessors in the extension host and a handful of orphaned
    getters and test seams; Preferences' "set home to current page"
    now asks the window for its current tab directly.
Memory and lifetime fixes (QUAL02):
    Engine tabs no longer leak their browsing profile — a profile
    created without an owner is now freed with its tab.  The Servo
    engine probe no longer leaks its registered backend object, and
    tab drag-and-drop no longer leaks its drag payload.  Closing or
    rebuilding an extension toolbar popup while its page was still
    evaluating could touch freed widgets — the resize callback now
    checks that both still exist first.  Sidebar notes stores are now
    bound to their profile's lifetime instead of the application's,
    so a discarded private profile takes its notes model with it.
Performance audit and reliability fixes (QUAL04):
    Measured baselines across startup, tab and paint paths are all
    inside their targets — cold start to first window ~200 ms, new
    tab ~1.5 ms, tab switch ~0.6 ms, themed chrome icons ~2 us per
    paint — so no speculative changes landed.  The History window no
    longer rebuilds a themed icon per row on every repaint.  An
    extension whose background worker dies is once more correctly
    reported "not supported": the hosted-runtime page shim no longer
    lands on the health probe's own page, and a stale queued probe can
    no longer overwrite a measured verdict.  The test suite now kills
    a hung test binary after a timeout instead of stalling the whole
    run.

Extension popups and pages get the hosted runtime too, and Bitwarden reaches its real login gate (EXT17/EXT18):
    Any extension document the browser renders — an action popup, an options page opened via chrome.runtime.openOptionsPage or tabs.create, an extension link in a tab — now gets the same DocumentCreation shim injection and its own chromeBridge channel wired to the hosted background's backend, so chrome.* calls from those pages work identically to the background's. The channel binds only same-profile, non-private extension pages; navigating across extension boundaries detaches the old bridge so contexts never cross. The bundled Bitwarden popup now boots its real Angular app end-to-end: the extension's i18n message map is embedded into its injected preamble (its flat lookup is what Angular's bootstrap awaited), its intro carousel clicks through, and the popup presents the genuine vault login gate — email and password inputs, "Log in with passkey", single-sign-on — verified headless. Remaining limits are honest rather than hidden: unlocking the vault itself requires real Bitwarden account credentials (server-authenticated, then client-side key derivation — nothing in the browser blocks it), chrome.tabs.sendMessage to content scripts and chrome.notifications answer typed errors that surface in the extension's health row, and the Settings details pane lists the degraded surfaces by name ("Enabled (hosted, degraded)") when the shim answers a typed error. chrome.extension.getBackgroundPage() returning null for a hosted service-worker background matches Chrome's own MV3 behavior.

A debug Servo build can no longer masquerade as "the engine is just slow" (ENG29):
    A tree carrying only an unoptimized target/debug servo-embed artifact (over a gigabyte of debug libservo) silently loaded it and pages crawled — the resolver preferred release but fell back to debug without telling anyone. Now a debug artifact still loads, but loudly: the app logs a one-time warning naming the artifact and the fix (`cargo build --release` in spikes/servo/servo-embed), the engine name carries a "(debug build)" marker everywhere it appears, and the engine surfaces — the toolbar engine button's tooltip and menu, the Preferences engine dropdown — show a caveat line. That same caveat carries the honest baseline on release builds too: Servo is experimental and page loads are slower than Chromium. qmake also warns at configure time when servo-embed artifacts exist but none is a release build.

The Servo start page is finally reachable (ENG30):
    Setting Servo as the default engine made every new tab come up blank — the tab was created but nothing ever told it to load a page — and swapping a tab between engines carried the SOURCE engine's branded start page along, so a Servo tab could show the Chromium-branded start page and vice versa. A fresh engine-default tab now loads that engine's branded start page, an engine swap re-resolves any branded start page (or the about:home alias) to the destination's own variant, and the same re-resolution applies when the Home button or a typed about:home targets a foreign-engine tab — the servo start page shows on Servo tabs, the plain one on Chromium, never crossed.
Development Tools menu entries are plain actions, not checkboxes (DEVT08/DEVT14):
    Tools → Development Tools showed a tick box on "BiDi Dev Tools" but not on "Chromium Dev Tool" — the BiDi row borrowed the dock's toggleViewAction, which Qt makes checkable by design. These entries open or focus separate tool surfaces; they are not persistent states, so neither should carry a check. The BiDi entry is now a dedicated plain action whose trigger flips the dock's visibility; the dock's own close button remains the other close path and a non-checkable action can never strand a stale tick. Tor windows keep the entry visible but disabled with its explanatory tooltip.
BiDi Dev Tools selector lists every tab, not just the debuggable ones (DEVT10):
    The context combo was fed only by browsingContext.getTree, which the
    CDP bridge filters to real page targets — engine tabs (Servo) and
    widget tabs (Preferences, Downloads) had no target and silently
    vanished, so a non-CDP tab in slot 0 read as 'the first tab is not
    selectable'. The panel now binds the window's actual tab strip:
    every tab gets a row in strip order, non-debuggable rows are
    disabled with the reason spelled out ('Fake Engine tab — no
    devtools backend', 'Not a web page — the devtools panel inspects
    web content only'), a sleeping or unexposed WebEngine tab says so
    instead of pretending, and CDP pages with no tab here (other
    windows, the inspector itself) keep appending at the end.
    Selection restores by context id and falls back to the first
    ENABLED row, so a strip whose slot 0 is a Servo or widget tab can
    no longer strand the selection on a dead entry — and an ordinary
    WebEngine tab in slot 0 selects and evaluates normally.
BiDi Dev Tools Network tab gains Chrome's toolbar, colored waterfall, and timing ruler (DEVT11):
    The Network tab read as a bare table with filter chips while
    Chromium's reads as one tool — the gap is now closed. The toolbar
    mirrors Chrome's row: a record/stop capture toggle (red dot while
    recording) and the clear button lead, the filter field stretches,
    and Preserve log / Disable cache trail; the two controls the debug
    channel cannot back — the throttling selector and response-body
    search — sit in their slots honestly disabled with tooltips that
    say why instead of pretending to work. Waterfall bars are colored
    by resource type (documents blue, stylesheets green, scripts
    amber, images purple, XHR/fetch light blue, and so on — failed
    requests stay red, pending ones stay translucent), a millisecond
    tick ruler runs across the top of the Waterfall column, and faint
    gridlines on the same ladder run behind the bars so every row
    lines up with the ruler. Hovering a bar breaks the request into
    Chrome's phase list — DNS, initial connection, SSL, request sent,
    waiting (TTFB), content download — showing only the phases the
    engine actually reported; cache-served rows grey out like Chrome's
    muted '(disk cache)' entries, and the Size hover now names both
    the wire bytes and the decoded resource size.
BiDi Dev Tools Console reads like Chromium's Console (DEVT12):
    The flat '[error] text' dump is gone — entries are structured rows
    colored by level (errors wash red, warnings amber, verbose dims,
    info/log stay plain, with no textual level prefix), each carrying
    its source url:line on the right edge when the engine reports it.
    A 'Default levels' dropdown toggles Errors/Warnings/Info/Verbose
    as a checkable set and a text field filters live; identical
    consecutive entries collapse into Chrome's repeat-count badge, and
    objects expand into preview children whenever the bridge payload
    carries structured arguments (the bridge now forwards the CDP
    arg previews and the message source for console calls, exceptions
    and log entries). The eval line sits on a real '>' prompt row with
    Up/Down history recall, a console-scoped Preserve log keeps
    entries across navigations, and the clear button empties the view.

Servo wheel scrolling is frame-paced instead of backlog-prone (ENG31):
    Sustained wheel scrolling on a Servo tab felt choppy because scroll deliveries ran on a fixed 16ms timer while every delivered delta costs a full-viewport software raster (about 34ms per frame on llvmpipe) — input queued up to twice as fast as frames could paint, so a burst ground through stale deltas while live input waited. Wheel deltas are now gated on frame completion: the first notch of a burst still goes out immediately, but while its frame is in flight later deltas accumulate and flush as one summed scroll per completed frame, locking input rate to paint rate and bounding the queue at a single delivery. A watchdog releases the gate when a delivered delta produces no frame at all (overscroll clamp or a deduped input), so scrolling never latches. The --servo-smoke scroll bench now reports both sides — se_wheel deliveries versus frames painted plus a frame-time histogram — and asserts that sends can never outrun paints by more than one in-flight delivery; a synchronous 20-notch burst now collapses to about 2 deliveries carrying the full summed distance. The honest ceiling stands: llvmpipe full-surface repaints cap Servo scrolling near 30fps on heavy pages — smoother than a stalled backlog, never Chromium-compositor smooth.

BiDi Dev Tools now carries Chrome's full top tab row (DEVT13):
    Elements, Console, Sources, Network, Performance, Memory,
    Application, Security — the old DOM and Storage tabs are gone,
    folded into Elements and Application. A new arora.cdp bridge
    command passes raw CDP calls onto the page session, so the panel
    probes each domain live and only ships what actually answers:
    Elements keeps the lazy DOM tree and adds Chrome's breadcrumb
    trail, a click-to-inspect highlight overlay, and a styles pane
    (real computed styles when the CSS domain responds, an honest
    attribute/inline-style readout when it does not); Sources lists
    the page's resources with a read-only syntax-highlighted view
    and working URL:line breakpoints through the Debugger domain;
    Performance shows a live metrics table plus CPU profiles
    summarized by self time; Memory aggregates allocation samples
    by function; Application grows a storage tree over cookies,
    local/session storage and IndexedDB; Security reports the page
    scheme, the TlsVerifier verdict and mixed-content counts from
    our own data. A domain that refuses reads as a disabled tab
    with the reason — never a fake.
Extension toolbar popups no longer render blank white (EXT19):
    Clicking an extension's toolbar action (e.g. Bitwarden) opened a popup that stayed white — the popup's shim preamble waited for a bridge channel that the live code path never attached. Extension pages now wire themselves: the hosted-extension runtime watches every web view the application raises and attaches the chromeBridge channel as soon as a chrome-extension:// document commits (and detaches it again on navigation away), so any popup, options page, or extension tab gets a working chrome.* surface without the embedder needing to know about it. If the extension's background context genuinely cannot be reached, the popup shows a real "extension background not running" panel instead of silent white, and keeps retrying so it recovers on its own when the background finishes starting.

Servo wheel scrolling uses the compositor fast path with smooth easing (ENG33):
    Wheel scrolling on a Servo tab still felt slow and "chubby" after frame pacing landed (ENG31): every delivered delta still round-tripped through Servo's script thread for DOM wheel dispatch before the scroll was allowed to run, each notch landed as a single hard jump, and rapid notches never accelerated. Wheel input now rides a new compositor-direct export in the servo-embed shim — se_scroll feeds the delta straight into the painter's scroll tree, where it lands as a webrender APZ scroll-offset transaction on the next update (no script round-trip, no re-layout, damage-limited repaint) — and an explicit event-loop wake in the shim keeps a queued scroll from stalling on an idle page, which libservo's queue-only notify_scroll_event would otherwise allow. On top of the faster path, each notch eases out across about 4–6 frames as proportional slices of the remaining distance (the shape of native smooth-scroll animations, capped well below a teleport), and repeated notches inside a short window ramp the multiplier to 3.5x like the system-scroll acceleration Chrome and Firefox apply — a flick covers real ground instead of crawling at one notch-distance per frame. Trade-off, documented in the shim and accepted for this experimental backend: DOM wheel listeners do not fire and preventDefault cannot veto on the fast path, so wheel-intercepting widgets degrade to a normal scroll; hosts on older artifacts keep the DOM-correct se_wheel route automatically. The --servo-smoke scroll bench gained largest-step and settle instrumentation plus slice/accel/pixel-delta legs; on the llvmpipe release build a lone notch now lands over ~5 paints instead of one jump and a rapid 6-notch flick covers roughly 2.3x the flat distance. The honest ceiling is unchanged: every frame is still a full-surface llvmpipe raster, so peak scroll rate stays bounded by paint cost.
- The compositor scroll path gained a live kill-switch (ENG34): setting
  ARORA_SERVO_SCROLL_PATH=wheel before launch returns Servo wheel input
  to the DOM-correct se_wheel route — DOM wheel listeners and
  preventDefault work again — for pages whose wheel-intercepting
  widgets regress on the fast path, without a rebuild.
"New Tor circuit for this site" now visibly rotates the circuit (TOR13):
    The panel's New-circuit button read as a no-op — SIGNAL NEWNYM only
    re-homes NEW streams, so the page kept riding the dirty old circuit
    and the busiest-circuit display kept showing the same hops. An
    acknowledged NEWNYM now reloads the site's tab (Tor Browser's flow)
    so its traffic lands on a fresh circuit, and the hop-chain display
    prefers circuits created after the request, so the rendered chain
    actually rotates. Clicks inside tor's ~10 s NEWNYM rate limit report
    the wait honestly instead of faking another rotation, and a
    greyed-out button now carries the reason on its tooltip.
- Lane sync (SYNC01): the rust-work branch absorbed 381 commits of
  master, so all of master's current features — packaging scripts
  (deb/rpm/flatpak), Picture-in-Picture plumbing, zoom wiring and the
  SourceViewer pipeline — are now present on this lane alongside the
  Rust components and Servo scroll work.
Inactive tabs now release memory without being reloaded (MEM06):
    The renderer kept every visited page for instant back/forward —
    measured at ~5 MB per navigation and never freed until the OS
    pressures it. Preferences → Performance → Memory Saver gains
    "Reclaim memory from inactive tabs every few minutes" (on by
    default): a short freeze pulse on each idle background tab makes
    the engine collect those pages while the tab stays loaded, so it
    never reloads when you return. Tabs that are loading, playing
    sound, or downloading are skipped. The status-bar memory figure
    now also says its number is memory the engine reclaims when the
    system needs it.
Memory coverage audit across every tab surface (MEM07):
    The article→back memory climb was measured per surface — normal,
    private, background, torn-off window, engine tab, a bare engine
    control, and a real Tor window whose page loads verifiably rode a
    SOCKS tunnel. Every surface shows the same ~4–8 MB/cycle renderer
    climb as the bare engine control, while the application's own
    memory stays essentially flat — confirming the growth is the web
    engine's reclaimable retention, not an Arora leak in any specific
    profile or interceptor path. The audit also fixed Tor mode
    force-upgrading plain HTTP on localhost/LAN addresses to HTTPS,
    which made local services unreachable in Tor windows.
Manual memory flush on the tab context menu (MEM08):
    The tab right-click menu gains "Free Memory" next to Sleep/Wake.
    On a background tab it discards the page outright — the only
    honest release of the renderer's per-navigation retention — while
    keeping the slot, title and favicon (the same machinery as Sleep
    Tab, including the unsaved-form refusal). The current tab can't
    stay discarded while visible, so its entry reads "Reload & Free
    Memory" and runs a discard+reactivate pulse that rebuilds the
    page's web contents and reloads the current entry — briefly, the
    tab's last painted frame stays on screen, so the only visible
    effect is the reload itself. Roughly two seconds later the status
    bar reports the measured result — "Freed ~N MB" from the tab's
    renderer RSS before and after — or "No memory freed" / "already
    asleep" when nothing moved. The same menu's "Free Memory in
    Background Tabs" flushes every quiet background tab at once
    (skipping the current tab, pinned tabs, loading/audible/
    downloading tabs and dirty forms).
Padlock verdict on Tor windows (TRUST23):
    The address-bar padlock used to sit amber on every Tor-window
    page: its green tier needs an independent TLS second opinion,
    and that probe is a direct handshake that can never run over Tor
    without de-anonymizing the user. The padlock now lets the
    engine's own chain validation stand in for the probe on Tor
    windows — TLS still rides the tunnel end-to-end, and a page only
    commits once the chain verifies — so a known, verified HTTPS site
    earns the same green it would on a normal window. Little-known
    sites stay honest amber, and one new red applies everywhere:
    proceeding past a certificate-error page means the committed
    chain FAILED the engine's check, so the padlock (and its popover,
    which now says so outright) renders red for the rest of the
    session.
BiDi Dev Tools tab selector re-matches instead of stranding pages on "no debug target" (DEVT16):
    A web tab that opened or finished loading while the BiDi panel's
    target snapshot was stale stayed disabled as "no debug target"
    forever — the selector compared the tab's url against a getTree
    snapshot that page navigations never refreshed, and even a fresh
    snapshot could disagree in spelling (typed "http://site" vs the
    committed "http://site/"). The selector now re-merges whenever
    the bridge reports a context's url moving, matches the two url
    forms through normalization, pairs a lone failed-navigation tab
    with its lone chrome-error target, and shows "attaching…" with a
    short bounded retry while the debug channel is live — "no debug
    target" is now the verdict only after the retries come back
    empty, so every real WebEngine tab ends up selectable.
BiDi Dev Tools Elements tab shows the selected page's real DOM tree (DEVT17):
    The Elements page could come up empty or stuck on "loading…" even
    though the page loaded fine: housekeeping replies from the debug
    channel were mistaken for the context list and silently wiped it,
    the tree only re-fetched at the start of a navigation — before the
    new document existed — a rebuilt tab selector could move to a
    different page without repopulating, and a refused or vanished
    fetch was dropped without a trace. The tree now re-fetches when
    the document actually finishes parsing (and once more at load if
    it is still empty), every selection change repopulates it, real
    replies can no longer clobber the tab list, and a failed fetch
    shows an honest error row that re-expanding retries.

BiDi Dev Tools Network waterfall stays on screen in narrow docks (DEVT18):
    The timing bars themselves were arriving fine — the Waterfall
    column was being pushed entirely off the right edge at real dock
    widths because the request table kept every fixed column at its
    preferred width before falling back to horizontal scrolling. Under
    pressure the fixed columns now give up their slack down to their
    floors, and the Initiator column yields before the graph can leave
    the viewport; it returns once the table fits it again.

Multi-profile foundation — data layout is now per-profile (MPROF01):
    The application's data directory gains a profiles/<id>/ layout
    with a profiles.json registry (id, display name, avatar, created
    timestamp; the id is an immutable filesystem slug). On first boot
    the existing single-profile stores — history (including the live
    SQLite WAL sidecars), bookmarks, autofill, adblock subscriptions,
    containers, userscripts, extension data, notes, secure-store keys,
    session and download parts — are moved into profiles/default/ by
    rename, never copy-then-delete, and a migration-v1.done marker
    records the clean pass; a partial move resumes on the next boot
    and a colliding payload is reported loudly instead of silently
    splitting state. Machine-global state (crash log, Tor bundle,
    QtWebEngine's own namespaced stores, the wipe marker, the
    registry itself) stays at the data root. There is no visible
    behavior change yet: every window still runs the default profile
    until the per-profile managers and window binding land.
Reader-mode button no longer paints blue on Tor windows (THEM06):
    On a Tor window's violet navigation bar the reader-mode icon kept
    the application palette's blue accent: its "suggested" outline+dot
    and its active page-fill both resolved QPalette::Accent, which the
    tor stylesheet never reseeds (a stylesheet repolish rebuilds the
    widget palette, so a palette-level seed cannot survive anyway).
    The button now carries an accent override applied to the icon's
    palette snapshot at paint time, and Tor windows set it to the tor
    raised violet — the same family the Tor badge pill and sidebar
    selection already wear. Suggested and active states stay
    distinguishable but in-theme; normal windows are unchanged.
Per-profile app singletons (MPROF02):
    The global qApp singletons — HistoryManager, BookmarksManager,
    AutoFillManager, CookieJar, AdBlockManager, ContainerManager,
    ExtensionManager, DownloadManager, NotesModel and SecureStore —
    are now keyed by registry profile: each gained an
    instanceFor(profileId) returning a qApp-owned per-persona
    instance (instance() still aliases the active profile, "default"
    today). A page's persona resolves through
    BrowserProfile::profileIdFor: the "aroraProfileId" tag the profile
    factories set wins, otherwise the storage name is parsed —
    "arora-<id>" names persona <id> while "arora-container-*" resolves
    through its owner tag — and unnamed, foreign or off-the-record
    profiles fall back to "default" so they can never alias a real
    persona's store. Process-global Rust stores (history database,
    site decisions, credential/autofill maps) are re-anchored to the
    caller's profiles/<id>/ directory before every entry point, so two
    live personas can never write into each other's stores. Site
    decisions split honestly: trust, permission and adblock-site
    verdicts stay on the shared default-profile store (trust is not
    persona data) while per-host cookie rules are per-persona. Tor and
    private profiles resolve to the default persona's managers —
    mode-level, not persona-level — and off-the-record pages feed
    nothing: history, notes, jars and autofill all stay volatile.
    Notes made on a page now land in that page's profile's notes
    store; the pre-split <data>/notes/<storageName>.json file is
    adopted into profiles/default/ on first read. Verified by the new
    tst_profilemanagers suite: two personas write disjoint stores,
    seeded history/bookmarks/notes/secrets stay invisible across ids,
    and the off-the-record legs confirm nothing persists.

Password-sealed profile vaults in the Rust core (PVAULT01):
    The rustcore crate gains a profilevault module behind the C ABI:
    an "ARV1" vault file packs a whole profile directory into a
    chunked, streamable, tamper-evident archive sealed under a
    password.  The vault key is a fresh random 512-bit enc+mac
    composite per seal, itself wrapped by an Argon2id-derived KEK
    (64MiB/t3/p4), so changing the password only re-wraps a ~200-byte
    header field — the payload never moves.  AES-256-GCM per 16MiB
    chunk plus HMAC-SHA-512 over the header and the whole payload is
    the default lane; an opt-in Threefish-1024 XEX-style lane with
    Skein-MAC chunk tags (algorithm agility — the RustCrypto crates
    are unaudited upstream, documented in-tree) rides the same
    container.  Errors stay honest and distinct: a wrong password is
    WrongPassword, a damaged vault is Tampered, an unknown algo_id is
    UnsupportedAlgo — and unseal stream-verifies the entire payload
    MAC before a single plaintext byte is written, extracting into a
    staging dir that only atomically renames into place on success,
    so a failed restore leaves zero partial output.  The FFI surface
    is rv_seal / rv_unseal / rv_verify / rv_inspect / rv_rewrap with a
    byte-progress callback for UI progress bars; the Qt-side profile
    export/import UI consumes it in a follow-up task.  No-Rust builds
    are unaffected.
